(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["dpadv",{"l:dpadv/how-does-interval-dp-solve-problems-over-every-c":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>When a problem keeps merging, splitting or removing pieces of a sequence, and a contiguous block interacts with the rest only through its ends, make the \u003cb>interval\u003c/b> the state: \u003ccode>dp[i][j]\u003c/code> is the best answer for the part i..j. Try every \u003cb>split point\u003c/b>, or every choice of the \u003cb>last action\u003c/b> inside the interval, combine two shorter intervals, and fill the table by \u003cb>increasing length\u003c/b> so both halves are ready. With n² intervals and up to n choices each, that is O(n³).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Multiplying four matrices A0 A1 A2 A3 gives the same result whatever you multiply first, but the work can differ by a factor of ten or more. Look at the \u003cb>last\u003c/b> multiplication performed: it joins the product of a left block with the product of a right block, for example (A0 A1)(A2 A3) or A0 (A1 A2 A3). Each block is the same problem on a shorter interval, and the two blocks never interact. Try every place for that final split, and you have the whole recurrence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The same shape appears whenever adjacent pieces combine: merging neighbouring piles, cutting a stick where each cut costs the current piece's length, bursting balloons whose neighbours then touch, parsing a sentence with a grammar, folding an RNA strand, and two players taking turns from either end of a row.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>State, recurrence and order\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Matrix Ai has shape \u003ccode>dims[i] × dims[i+1]\u003c/code>. Multiplying a p × q matrix by a q × r one costs p·q·r scalar multiplications, so:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>dp[i][i] = 0\ndp[i][j] = min over k in [i, j) of  dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + dims[i]·dims[k+1]·dims[j+1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>dp[i][j]\u003c/code> reads only strictly shorter intervals, so filling by length (all intervals of length 2, then 3, and so on) always finds its inputs ready. Equivalent orders are i from n - 1 down to 0 with j from i upwards, or a memoised recursion, which finds an order by itself. Recording the best k per interval lets you rebuild the bracketing at the end.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Split point or last action?\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Sometimes the obvious choice breaks the problem. Remove one balloon from a row and its neighbours become adjacent, so the left and right parts now interact: choosing the \u003cb>first\u003c/b> balloon to burst does not split the problem. Choose instead the balloon burst \u003cb>last\u003c/b> inside an open interval (l, r): while everything else between l and r disappears, that balloon separates the two sides, and when it finally bursts its neighbours are known, namely l and r. The rule of thumb: pick the action whose context is fixed when it happens, which is usually the last one.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Game intervals\u003c/h5>\u003cp>When two players alternately take from either end of a row, let \u003ccode>dp[i][j]\u003c/code> be the best score \u003cb>difference\u003c/b> the player to move can force on i..j: \u003ccode>max(a[i] - dp[i+1][j], a[j] - dp[i][j-1])\u003c/code>. Storing the difference lets both players use the same formula. There is no split loop, so this costs O(n²).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are n(n+1)/2 intervals and each tries up to n splits: \u003cb>O(n³) time, O(n²) space\u003c/b>. In CPython, n = 300 took about 2 seconds for the matrix chain, so n around 500 is the practical ceiling without vectorising the inner loop. Interval games without a split loop are O(n²). Some interval DPs with special structure (optimal binary search trees, certain merging costs) drop to O(n²) with Knuth's optimisation; it is enough to know that it exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cues: \"merge adjacent\", \"burst\" or \"remove\" with neighbours joining, \"cut into pieces\" with a cost depending on the current piece, \"parenthesise\", \"take from either end\", n at most a few hundred. Not interval DP when the chosen items need not be contiguous (that is a subset: bitmask DP or knapsack), or when every decision extends a prefix (a 1D DP suffices).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>dims = [10, 30, 5, 60, 10]: A0 is 10 × 30, A1 is 30 × 5, A2 is 5 × 60, A3 is 60 × 10. Blank cells are not filled yet.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Length 1: a single matrix needs no multiplication, so the diagonal is 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>      j=0     j=1     j=2     j=3\ni=0     0\ni=1             0\ni=2                     0\ni=3                             0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Length 2: one way each. [0,1] = 10·30·5 = 1500, [1,2] = 30·5·60 = 9000, [2,3] = 5·60·10 = 3000\u003c/p>\u003cpre>      j=0     j=1     j=2     j=3\ni=0     0   \u003cmark>1500\u003c/mark>\ni=1             0   \u003cmark>9000\u003c/mark>\ni=2                     0   \u003cmark>3000\u003c/mark>\ni=3                             0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Length 3, [0,2]: split after A0 costs 0 + 9000 + 10·30·60 = 27000; after A1 costs 1500 + 0 + 10·5·60 = 4500. Keep 4500\u003c/p>\u003cpre>      j=0     j=1     j=2     j=3\ni=0     0    1500   \u003cmark>4500\u003c/mark>\ni=1             0    9000\ni=2                     0    3000\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Length 3, [1,3]: after A1 costs 0 + 3000 + 30·5·10 = 4500; after A2 costs 9000 + 0 + 30·60·10 = 27000. Keep 4500\u003c/p>\u003cpre>      j=0     j=1     j=2     j=3\ni=0     0    1500    4500\ni=1             0    9000   \u003cmark>4500\u003c/mark>\ni=2                     0    3000\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Length 4, [0,3]: k=0 gives 0 + 4500 + 3000 = 7500, k=1 gives 1500 + 3000 + 500 = 5000, k=2 gives 4500 + 0 + 6000 = 10500. Best: (A0 A1)(A2 A3) for 5000\u003c/p>\u003cpre>      j=0     j=1     j=2     j=3\ni=0     0    1500    4500   \u003cmark>5000\u003c/mark>\ni=1             0    9000    4500\ni=2                     0    3000\ni=3                             0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Multiplying strictly left to right, ((A0 A1) A2) A3, would cost 1500 + 3000 + 6000 = 10500, more than twice the optimum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def matrix_chain(dims):\n    \"\"\"Fewest scalar multiplications for A0 A1 ... A(n-1), where Ai is dims[i] x dims[i+1].\n    Returns (cost, split) where split[i][j] is the best last split point for Ai..Aj.\"\"\"\n    n = len(dims) - 1\n    cost = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    split = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):               # shorter intervals first\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1\n            cost[i][j] = float(\"inf\")\n            for k in range(i, j):                # last product: (Ai..Ak) times (Ak+1..Aj)\n                c = cost[i][k] + cost[k + 1][j] + dims[i] * dims[k + 1] * dims[j + 1]\n                if c &lt; cost[i][j]:\n                    cost[i][j], split[i][j] = c, k\n    return cost[0][n - 1], split\n\n\ndef parenthesise(split, i, j):\n    if i == j:\n        return f\"A{i}\"\n    k = split[i][j]\n    return f\"({parenthesise(split, i, k)} {parenthesise(split, k + 1, j)})\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>parenthesise(split, 0, 3)\u003c/code> on the trace returns \u003ccode>((A0 A1) (A2 A3))\u003c/code>. To adapt the template, change three things: what an interval means (closed [i, j] here; open (l, r) for balloons), the choices inside it, and the cost of combining.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Row-by-row filling.\u003c/b> Computing \u003ccode>dp[0][3]\u003c/code> before \u003ccode>dp[1][3]\u003c/code> reads a cell that is still 0 and silently returns a wrong minimum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Open versus closed intervals.\u003c/b> With padding (balloons, cut points including both stick ends) the interval is open and k ranges strictly between l and r; mixing conventions gives off-by-one errors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The first action instead of the last.\u003c/b> If removing a piece changes its neighbours, a \"first action\" recurrence does not decompose; switch to the last action.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recomputing range sums.\u003c/b> When the combine cost is the sum of a[i..j], precompute prefix sums or the O(n³) becomes O(n⁴).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python speed.\u003c/b> O(n³) with n = 500 is about 2·10^7 inner steps, several seconds in CPython; cache rows in locals or vectorise the inner loop with NumPy.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Fill the table for dims = [5, 10, 3, 12] (three matrices). What is the minimum cost and the bracketing?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>[0,1] = 5·10·3 = 150, [1,2] = 10·3·12 = 360. For [0,2], splitting after A0 costs 0 + 360 + 5·10·12 = 960, after A1 costs 150 + 0 + 5·3·12 = 330. Minimum 330, as (A0 A1) A2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does choosing the last balloon burst in (l, r) make the two sides independent, when choosing the first does not?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If balloon k bursts last in (l, r), it is still present while everything in (l, k) and (k, r) bursts, so it acts as a wall: each side only ever sees its own balloons plus the fixed ends (l and k, or k and r). Its own coins are then a[l]·a[k]·a[r], known exactly. If k burst first, l's side and r's side would become neighbours afterwards, and the coins on each side would depend on the other.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Adjacent piles of stones are merged two at a time, each merge costing the size of the new pile. Write the recurrence, and give the answer for piles [4, 1, 3, 2].\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>dp[i][j] = min over k of dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + sum(a[i..j])\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>dp[i][i] = 0\u003c/code>, using prefix sums for the range sum. For [4, 1, 3, 2] the answer is 20: merge 1 and 3 (4), then that pile with 2 (6), then with 4 (10).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>486 Predict the Winner.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>dp[i][j]\u003c/code> is the best difference for the player to move; player 1 wins on a difference of at least 0. Brute force first: plain recursion without memo, exponential. Expected: O(n²) time, O(n²) or O(n) space; \u003ccode>[2, 9, 4]\u003c/code> gives false (difference -3) and \u003ccode>[2, 9, 4, 1]\u003c/code> gives true (difference 4).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1547 Minimum Cost to Cut a Stick.\u003c/b> Sort the cuts, add both ends, and run interval DP over consecutive cut positions. Brute force: try every order of cuts (m!) for small m. Expected: O(m³) for m cuts; \u003ccode>n = 10, cuts = [2, 4, 7]\u003c/code> gives 20, and \u003ccode>n = 8, cuts = [1, 6, 3, 5]\u003c/code> gives 19.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>312 Burst Balloons\u003c/b> (derive). Pad with 1s and choose the last balloon in each open interval. Brute force: try every burst order. Expected: O(n³); \u003ccode>[4, 2, 6, 1]\u003c/code> gives 84 and \u003ccode>[7]\u003c/code> gives 7.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to set up interval DP, fill it by increasing length, and pick a split point or the last action so that the subproblems are independent.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:dpadv/how-do-you-run-dp-on-a-tree-and-how-does-rerooti":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A rooted tree is already a DAG of subproblems: a node's answer depends only on its children's answers. Give each node a small tuple of states, such as \"best if this node is chosen\" and \"best if it is not\", compute children before parents (\u003cb>post-order\u003c/b>), and combine. For paths through a node, join its \u003cb>two best child chains\u003c/b>. To answer for \u003cb>every\u003c/b> possible root, \u003cb>reroot\u003c/b>: solve once for root 0, then move the root across one edge at a time, adjusting the answer in O(1). Everything is O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Plan an office party where nobody attends together with their direct manager. Each manager waits for every team below to report two numbers, \"the best total if my team lead comes\" and \"the best total if not\", and then works out the same two numbers for their own team. The reports flow up the hierarchy; the chief executive's two numbers decide the answer. No manager ever needs to look more than one level down, because each report already summarises a whole subtree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>States per node, children first\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Example: place the fewest guards on junctions so that every road (tree edge) has a guard at one of its ends (a minimum vertex cover). For each node v:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>guard[v] = 1 + sum over children c of min(guard[c], free[c])\nfree[v]  =     sum over children c of guard[c]          (an unguarded v forces every child road to be guarded at c)\nanswer   = min(guard[root], free[root])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The maximum-weight version where adjacent nodes cannot both be chosen (house robber on a tree) has the same skeleton: \u003ccode>take[v] = val[v] + sum skip[c]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>skip[v] = sum max(take[c], skip[c])\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To get children before parents without recursion, list the nodes in BFS or DFS order from the root (parents before children) and process that list backwards.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Paths through a node\u003c/h5>\u003cp>For \"longest path\" style questions, let each node return the longest chain going \u003cb>down\u003c/b> from it. The best path that turns at v joins its two longest child chains: \u003ccode>1 + top1 + top2\u003c/code>. Track the global maximum over all nodes; return \u003ccode>1 + top1\u003c/code> to the parent. Constraints such as \"adjacent nodes must differ\" just decide which children may extend v's chain; every child is still processed for its own subtree.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Rerooting\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Some questions ask for an answer per possible root: the total distance from each node to all others, the farthest node from each node, or this one: roads in a tree are one-way, and for every city you want the number of roads to reverse so that everyone can drive to it. Naively that is one O(n) pass per root, O(n²) in total. Instead:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>Root at 0 and compute \u003ccode>ans[0]\u003c/code>: the number of roads pointing \u003cb>away\u003c/b> from 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Moving the root from p to a child v changes the meaning of exactly one road, the one between p and v. If it ran p -&gt; v it counted for p but points towards v, so \u003ccode>ans[v] = ans[p] - 1\u003c/code>; if it ran v -&gt; p, it now points away from the new root, so \u003ccode>ans[v] = ans[p] + 1\u003c/code>. Every other road keeps its direction relative to the root.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Walking the tree top-down applies this rule along every edge: O(n) for all roots. For harder answers, the shift needs some subtree information from the first pass (sizes, best chains), but the plan is the same: one pass up, one pass down.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each edge is used once as a parent-child link, so the upward pass costs O(n·k) for k states per node, and rerooting adds one more O(n) pass: \u003cb>O(n) time and space\u003c/b> for a fixed number of states. Top-two tracking costs O(degree) per node, still O(n) in total. The naive alternative, recomputing from every root, is O(n²): 10^10 steps at n = 10^5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cues: the input is a tree (n - 1 edges, a parent array, or a binary tree), a rule ties a node to its parent or children (not both chosen, colours differ), the answer is a best path inside a tree, or the question says \"for every node\". Not for general graphs: maximum independent set or minimum vertex cover on a graph with cycles is NP-hard, so the tree shape is what makes this fast.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Fewest guards on this tree (root 0). Processing order: 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0 (BFS order reversed).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>            0\n         /  |  \\\n        1   2   3\n       / \\       \\\n      4   5       6\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Leaves 6, 5, 4 and 2: a guard covers nothing below, so guard = 1; with no children, free = 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node    0    1    2    3    4    5    6\nguard   .    .   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    .   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nfree    .    .   \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>    .   \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Node 3 (child 6): guard = 1 + min(1, 0) = 1; free = guard[6] = 1, because road 3-6 then needs a guard at 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node    0    1    2    3    4    5    6\nguard   .    .    1   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    1    1    1\nfree    .    .    0   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    0    0    0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Node 1 (children 4, 5): guard = 1 + 0 + 0 = 1; free = 1 + 1 = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node    0    1    2    3    4    5    6\nguard   .   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    1    1    1    1    1\nfree    .   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    0    1    0    0    0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Root 0 (children 1, 2, 3): guard = 1 + min(1,2) + min(1,0) + min(1,1) = 3; free = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3. Answer: 3 guards, for example at 0, 1 and 3, or at 1, 2 and 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node    0    1    2    3    4    5    6\nguard  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>    1    1    1    1    1    1\nfree   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>    2    0    1    0    0    0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def min_guards(n, edges, root=0):\n    \"\"\"Fewest guarded junctions so every road (tree edge) has a guard at one end.\"\"\"\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for a, b in edges:\n        adj[a].append(b)\n        adj[b].append(a)\n    parent = [-1] * n\n    order = [root]                               # preorder: parents before children\n    for u in order:\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            if v != parent[u]:\n                parent[v] = u\n                order.append(v)\n    guard = [1] * n                              # best for u's subtree if u has a guard\n    free = [0] * n                               # best for u's subtree if u has none\n    for u in reversed(order):                    # children before parents\n        p = parent[u]\n        if p != -1:\n            guard[p] += min(guard[u], free[u])\n            free[p] += guard[u]                  # an unguarded parent forces a guarded child\n    return min(guard[root], free[root])\n\n\ndef reversals_for_every_capital(n, roads):\n    \"\"\"roads: one-way (a, b) pairs forming a tree (drive a -&gt; b). For every city c, how many\n    roads must be reversed so every city can drive to c. Rerooting: two passes, O(n).\"\"\"\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for a, b in roads:\n        adj[a].append((b, 1))                    # stepping a -&gt; b follows the road\n        adj[b].append((a, 0))                    # stepping b -&gt; a goes against it\n    parent, away = [-1] * n, [0] * n             # away[v]: the road runs parent[v] -&gt; v\n    order = [0]\n    for u in order:                              # preorder from root 0\n        for v, along in adj[u]:\n            if v != parent[u]:\n                parent[v], away[v] = u, along\n                order.append(v)\n    ans = [0] * n\n    ans[0] = sum(away)                           # roads pointing away from root 0 must flip\n    for v in order[1:]:                          # move the root across one edge at a time\n        ans[v] = ans[parent[v]] + (-1 if away[v] else 1)\n    return ans\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Appending to \u003ccode>order\u003c/code> while iterating over it is a compact BFS: the loop visits every node exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recursion depth.\u003c/b> A path-shaped tree of 10^5 nodes recurses 10^5 deep; Python's default limit is 1000. Use the order-list pattern, or raise the limit only for modest depths.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Walking back to the parent.\u003c/b> An undirected edge list must be rooted: skip the parent (by tracking it), or the \"children\" include the node you came from.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unclear state meaning.\u003c/b> Write one sentence per state (\"best total in v's subtree if v is guarded\") before coding the combine step; most tree DP bugs are mixed meanings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Top-two chains.\u003c/b> Start both at 0, and remember that a child excluded by a rule (same label, blocked edge) contributes nothing to v's chain but still needs its own subtree answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rerooting shifts.\u003c/b> The O(1) update must remove exactly the moved child's contribution and add the parent side's; test it against the naive \"solve from every root\" on small random trees.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give node 2 a child 7 in the trace tree. What are guard[2], free[2] and the final answer?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Node 7 is a leaf (guard 1, free 0), so guard[2] = 1 + min(1, 0) = 1 and free[2] = guard[7] = 1. At the root, guard = 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4 and free = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, so the answer is 3: guards at 1, 2 and 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must the nodes be processed in post-order (children first)?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A node's states are sums or extremes over its children's states. If a parent is combined before a child is finished, it reads the child's initial values, and that error is never revisited because each node is combined exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">With roads 0 -&gt; 1, 2 -&gt; 1 and 1 -&gt; 3, what does \u003ccode>reversals_for_every_capital\u003c/code> return, and why is city 3 a perfect capital?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>[2, 1, 2, 0]. Every road already points towards 3 (0 and 2 drive into 1, and 1 drives into 3), so nothing needs reversing; capital 0 needs 0 -&gt; 1 and 1 -&gt; 3 flipped, which is 2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>337 House Robber III.\u003c/b> Return a pair (rob this node, skip this node) from each subtree. Brute force: try every subset of nodes for tiny trees. Expected: O(n) time, O(h) stack; the tree \u003ccode>[4,1,5,2,3,null,1]\u003c/code> gives 10, and \u003ccode>[2,7,1,1,null,null,9]\u003c/code> gives 16.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2246 Longest Path With Different Adjacent Characters.\u003c/b> Each node returns its longest valid downward chain and combines its two best children whose letters differ from its own. Brute force: check the path between every pair of nodes. Expected: O(n); \u003ccode>parent = [-1,0,0,1,1,2,2], s = \"abbacca\"\u003c/code> gives 5, \u003ccode>parent = [-1,0,1,2], s = \"abab\"\u003c/code> gives 4, and \u003ccode>parent = [-1,0,0], s = \"aaa\"\u003c/code> gives 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>834 Sum of Distances in Tree\u003c/b> (derive). First pass: subtree sizes and the root's total; second pass: shift the total across each edge. Brute force: BFS from every node, O(n²). Expected: O(n); \u003ccode>n = 5, edges = [[0,1],[1,2],[1,3],[3,4]]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>[8, 5, 8, 6, 9]\u003c/code>, and the star \u003ccode>n = 4, edges = [[0,1],[0,2],[0,3]]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>[3, 5, 5, 5]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to run DP on a tree children-first, combine the best two child chains for paths, and reroot to answer for every node in O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:dpadv/how-does-bitmask-dp-turn-n-orderings-into-2-n-su":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Many ordering problems care only about \u003cb>which\u003c/b> items are used so far and \u003cb>where you are now\u003c/b>, not about the order in which you used them. Encode the used set as an integer whose bit i means \"item i is used\" and make (mask, last) the state. The travelling salesman over n cities then drops from (n - 1)! orders to 2^n · n states with n choices each: \u003cb>O(2^n · n²)\u003c/b>, the Held-Karp algorithm. That is practical up to n of about 20 in C++ and about 15 to 16 in Python, and memory, 2^n · n entries, is usually the real limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A delivery driver at 3 pm has visited shops A, C and D and is now standing at D. For planning the rest of the day, it does not matter whether the morning went A, C, D or C, A, D: only the set {A, C, D}, the current shop D and the distance driven so far matter. So of those two histories, keep the cheaper and forget the other. Every set of permutations with the same \"visited set and current position\" collapses into one state. There are n! orders but only 2^n · n such states, and that gap is the whole trick: for n = 15, about 8.7·10^10 tours from a fixed start against fewer than 500,000 states.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Masks in Python\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Operation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Code\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>is item i in the set?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>mask &gt;&gt; i &amp; 1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>add item i\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>mask | 1 &lt;&lt; i\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>remove item i\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>mask &amp; ~(1 &lt;&lt; i)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>size of the set\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>mask.bit_count()\u003c/code> (Python 3.10+) or \u003ccode>bin(mask).count(\"1\")\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>every item\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>(1 &lt;&lt; n) - 1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Adding an item makes the number larger, so looping over masks in increasing order always handles a set before any of its supersets: a valid DP order for free.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The travelling salesman (Held-Karp)\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>best[mask][v]\u003c/code> is the shortest route that starts at city 0, visits exactly the cities in mask, and ends at v. Start with \u003ccode>best[{0}][0] = 0\u003c/code>. From each state, extend to every unvisited city u: \u003ccode>best[mask | u][u] = min(..., best[mask][v] + d[v][u])\u003c/code>. The tour length is the minimum over v of \u003ccode>best[full][v] + d[v][0]\u003c/code>. Fixing city 0 as the start is safe because a tour is a cycle; it also halves the masks (only odd ones contain city 0). Storing the predecessor of each state lets you walk the tour back.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Other shapes\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Assignment.\u003c/b> n workers and n jobs: with jobs taken given by mask, the next worker to place is number \u003ccode>popcount(mask)\u003c/code>, so the state is the mask alone: O(2^n · n). Sum instead of minimising and you count perfect matchings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Packing into bins.\u003c/b> \"Fewest trips or sessions\": let \u003ccode>dp[mask]\u003c/code> be the pair (trips used, load of the current trip), minimised lexicographically; adding an item either fits in the current trip or opens a new one. O(2^n · n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Covering.\u003c/b> \"Smallest team covering every skill\": the mask is the set of skills covered, and each person ORs in their skills.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Partitions into groups.\u003c/b> Iterate the submasks of a mask with \u003ccode>s = (s - 1) &amp; mask\u003c/code>; over all masks this costs O(3^n).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Held-Karp has 2^n · n states and n transitions each: \u003cb>O(2^n · n²) time, O(2^n · n) space\u003c/b>. For n = 15 that is about 7·10^6 inner steps, which took under a second in CPython, while brute force over permutations took 15 seconds already at n = 11 (3.6 million tours). Each extra city roughly doubles the time: the template took 1.8 s at n = 16 and 7.6 s at n = 18. At n = 20 the count is about 4·10^8 steps (half that with city 0 fixed): around a second in C++, well over half a minute in CPython. Memory: the two tables (\u003ccode>best\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code>) peaked at about 15 MB for n = 15 and 136 MB for n = 18; at n = 20 one table has 21 million entries, about 170 MB of list pointers alone in Python, or 84 MB as 32-bit integers in C++. Estimate this before you allocate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cues: n at most about 20, and the task is to visit, assign, order, cover or group \u003cb>all\u003c/b> of a small set of items; the decisions so far can be summarised by \"which ones are used\" plus a little extra. Not for n of 25 or more (2^25 · 25 is about 8·10^8 states): reach for heuristics, branch and bound or an integer programming solver. Not when the set can be summarised by a count, as in \"how many items taken\" (that is ordinary DP).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Four cities, symmetric distances: d(0,1) = 10, d(0,2) = 15, d(0,3) = 20, d(1,2) = 35, d(1,3) = 25, d(2,3) = 30. Masks are written as sets and in binary (city 0 is the lowest bit).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Only city 0 visited, standing at 0, distance 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>{0}        0001   end 0: \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sets of size 2: go straight from 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>{0,1}      0011   end 1: \u003cmark>10\u003c/mark>\n{0,2}      0101   end 2: \u003cmark>15\u003c/mark>\n{0,3}      1001   end 3: \u003cmark>20\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Size 3: each entry keeps the cheaper of the two orders. {0,1,2} ending at 2: 0 1 2 = 10+35 = 45; ending at 1: 0 2 1 = 15+35 = 50\u003c/p>\u003cpre>{0,1,2}    0111   end 1: 50    end 2: \u003cmark>45\u003c/mark>\n{0,1,3}    1011   end 1: 45    end 3: \u003cmark>35\u003c/mark>   (0 1 3 = 10+25)\n{0,2,3}    1101   end 2: 50    end 3: \u003cmark>45\u003c/mark>   (0 2 3 = 15+30)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">All four: end 1 = min(50+35, 45+25) = 70; end 2 = min(45+35, 35+30) = 65; end 3 = min(50+25, 45+30) = 75\u003c/p>\u003cpre>{0,1,2,3}  1111   end 1: 70    end 2: \u003cmark>65\u003c/mark>    end 3: 75\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Close the tour with the edge back to 0: 70+10 = 80, 65+15 = 80, 75+20 = 95. Shortest tour: 80, for example 0 2 3 1 0 (or its reverse 0 1 3 2 0)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>end 1: 80    end 2: \u003cmark>80\u003c/mark>    end 3: 95\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Twelve (mask, end) entries replaced the six orderings of cities 1, 2, 3; at n = 15 it is 491,520 entries against 87 billion orderings.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def tsp(dist):\n    \"\"\"Held-Karp: length of the shortest tour from city 0 through every city and back,\n    plus the tour itself. O(2^n n^2) time, O(2^n n) space.\"\"\"\n    n = len(dist)\n    INF = float(\"inf\")\n    full = 1 &lt;&lt; n\n    best = [[INF] * n for _ in range(full)]      # best[mask][v]: path from 0 over mask, ending at v\n    prev = [[-1] * n for _ in range(full)]\n    best[1][0] = 0\n    for mask in range(1, full, 2):               # odd masks: city 0 is always visited\n        for v in range(n):\n            here = best[mask][v]\n            if here == INF:\n                continue\n            for u in range(n):\n                if mask &gt;&gt; u &amp; 1:\n                    continue\n                nxt = mask | 1 &lt;&lt; u\n                if here + dist[v][u] &lt; best[nxt][u]:\n                    best[nxt][u] = here + dist[v][u]\n                    prev[nxt][u] = v\n    last = min(range(n), key=lambda v: best[full - 1][v] + dist[v][0])\n    length = best[full - 1][last] + dist[last][0]\n    tour, mask = [], full - 1\n    while last != -1:                            # walk the choices back\n        tour.append(last)\n        mask, last = mask ^ (1 &lt;&lt; last), prev[mask][last]\n    return length, tour[::-1] + [0]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>On the trace, \u003ccode>tsp\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>(80, [0, 2, 3, 1, 0])\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Operator precedence in C, C++ and Java.\u003c/b> There, \u003ccode>mask &amp; 1 &lt;&lt; i == 0\u003c/code> means \u003ccode>mask &amp; ((1 &lt;&lt; i) == 0)\u003c/code>, because \u003ccode>==\u003c/code> binds tighter than \u003ccode>&amp;\u003c/code>; write \u003ccode>(mask &gt;&gt; i &amp; 1) == 0\u003c/code>. Python's comparisons bind more loosely than \u003ccode>&amp;\u003c/code>, so the same line works in Python, which makes ports dangerous.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Allocating before estimating.\u003c/b> Multiply 2^n by the per-state size first; for large n use \u003ccode>array\u003c/code>, NumPy, or a rolling layer per set size.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Path versus tour.\u003c/b> A Hamiltonian path that may end anywhere takes the minimum of \u003ccode>best[full][v]\u003c/code> without the closing edge; a tour must add \u003ccode>d[v][0]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Not fixing the start.\u003c/b> Letting every city start multiplies the work by n for no benefit when the answer is a cycle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Brute force as an oracle.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>itertools.permutations\u003c/code> over n - 1 cities is a perfect test oracle up to n = 9 or 10, and useless beyond.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the trace, where does \u003ccode>best[{0,1,2,3}][2] = 65\u003c/code> come from, and which candidate was discarded?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>From \u003ccode>best[{0,1,3}][3] = 35\u003c/code> (route 0 1 3) plus d(3,2) = 30, giving the route 0 1 3 2. The other candidate, \u003ccode>best[{0,1,3}][1] = 45\u003c/code> (route 0 3 1) plus d(1,2) = 35 = 80, was discarded, and no later state can ever need it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is it safe to keep only the cheapest history for each (set, end) pair?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every way of finishing the tour depends only on which cities remain (the complement of the set) and where you stand (the end city). Replace a history with a cheaper one for the same set and end and every completion gets cheaper by the same amount, so a more expensive history can never be part of an optimal tour.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Someone wants an exact tour for 18 cities in Python. Estimate the cost.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>2^18 · 18 · 18 is about 8.5·10^7 inner steps, half that with city 0 fixed: the template above took about 8 seconds and 136 MB in CPython, and C++ needs well under a second. Feasible for one run in Python; for repeated runs use arrays instead of lists of lists, or C++.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1986 Minimum Number of Work Sessions to Finish the Tasks.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>dp[mask]\u003c/code> = (sessions used, time used in the current session), minimised lexicographically. Brute force: assign each task to one of n sessions (n^n) for n up to 6. Expected: O(2^n · n); \u003ccode>tasks = [4,3,3,2,2,1], sessionTime = 5\u003c/code> gives 3, and \u003ccode>tasks = [6,2,2,2], sessionTime = 6\u003c/code> gives 2.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>AtCoder DP O, Matching.\u003c/b> Man number \u003ccode>popcount(mask)\u003c/code> is paired next; sum the counts modulo 10^9 + 7. Expected: O(2^N · N); the compatibility matrix \u003ccode>[[1,1,0],[1,1,1],[0,1,1]]\u003c/code> gives 3, the all-ones 4 × 4 matrix gives 24, and \u003ccode>[[1,0],[1,0]]\u003c/code> gives 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>943 Find the Shortest Superstring\u003c/b> (stretch). Precompute pairwise overlaps, then a (mask, last) DP that maximises total overlap, and rebuild the order. Expected: O(n² · 2^n + n² · L); \u003ccode>[\"dcab\",\"abxy\",\"xyz\"]\u003c/code> gives a 7-letter answer (\"dcabxyz\"), and \u003ccode>[\"pq\",\"qr\",\"rp\"]\u003c/code> a 4-letter one such as \"pqrp\".\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to use subsets as DP states, rebuild the best order, and decide from n whether 2^n · n states fit in time and memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:dpadv/travelling-salesman-for-15-cities-permutations-v":"\u003cp>You will hit the exponential wall twice, once at n! and once at 2^n · n², and measure exactly where each one stops you. Use the stress-test loop and doubling timer from your \u003ccode>dsakit\u003c/code> if you built it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import itertools\nimport math\nimport random\n\n\ndef cities(n, seed):\n    \"\"\"n random points in a 100 x 100 square; distances rounded to 3 decimals.\"\"\"\n    rnd = random.Random(seed)\n    pts = [(rnd.uniform(0, 100), rnd.uniform(0, 100)) for _ in range(n)]\n    return [[round(math.dist(a, b), 3) for b in pts] for a in pts]\n\n\ndef brute_force(d):\n    \"\"\"Shortest tour from city 0 by trying every order of the other cities.\"\"\"\n    best = float(\"inf\")\n    for p in itertools.permutations(range(1, len(d))):\n        tour = (0,) + p + (0,)\n        best = min(best, sum(d[a][b] for a, b in zip(tour, tour[1:])))\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Copy \u003ccode>tsp\u003c/code> from the bitmask card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Verify first: for n from 2 to 9, run 50 random instances each and assert that \u003ccode>brute_force\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>tsp\u003c/code> agree (compare floats with a tolerance of 1e-6), that the returned tour visits every city once, and that its length matches the reported one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Time \u003ccode>brute_force\u003c/code> for n = 4 to 11 and \u003ccode>tsp\u003c/code> for n = 4 to 16 (\u003ccode>time.perf_counter\u003c/code>, best of three for small n). Print one table and mark the crossover.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compute the ratio between consecutive times for each method. Predict the ratios before looking: brute force should grow by about n per step, the DP by a little over 2.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use your n = 11 brute-force time to predict how long brute force would take at n = 15.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Measure peak memory of \u003ccode>tsp\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>tracemalloc\u003c/code> for n = 12 to 16, in a separate run from the timings (tracemalloc slows Python down several times).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write a nearest-neighbour heuristic (always drive to the closest unvisited city) and record its gap to the optimum over 30 instances with n = 12.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional: halve the memory by storing only odd masks (index \u003ccode>mask &gt;&gt; 1\u003c/code>), or by using \u003ccode>array(\"d\")\u003c/code> rows, and measure again.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The two methods agree on every instance; if not, check the closing edge back to city 0 and the n = 1 and n = 2 cases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The crossover is tiny: around n = 6 the DP is already faster (about 1 ms each). At n = 9 brute force took about 0.26 s against about 0.01 s for the DP; at n = 11, about 15 s against 0.02 s.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Brute force grows by roughly n per step (×7 to ×10 here); the DP by about ×2 to ×2.5 per step, reaching under a second at n = 15 and about 2 s at n = 16.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Predicted brute force at n = 15: 15 s × (14!/10!) = 15 s × 24,024, about 4 days.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Peak memory for n = 15 is about 15 MB; each extra city roughly doubles it (and n = 18 reached about 136 MB).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Nearest neighbour is instant but was 14% longer than optimal on average over 30 instances, and 40% longer in the worst one: exact DP is worth having while n is small.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-note\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Note\u003c/p>\u003cp>For dozens or thousands of cities nobody solves TSP exactly with DP; real routing uses heuristics and local search (2-opt, Lin-Kernighan) or integer programming solvers. The DP is the exact baseline you check those heuristics against on small instances.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:dpadv/how-do-you-count-the-numbers-up-to-n-that-have-a":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>To count the x in [0, N] whose digits have some property, build x from the left one digit at a time and remember only three things: the \u003cb>position\u003c/b>, a small \u003cb>summary\u003c/b> of the digits so far (their sum, a remainder, the last digit, the set of digits used), and a \u003cb>tight\u003c/b> flag saying whether the prefix still equals N's prefix, which caps the next digit at N's digit. Once a prefix drops below N's, the remaining digits are free, and the count depends only on (position, summary), so memoisation shares it across every such prefix. Answer a range as f(R) - f(L - 1).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the numbers from 0 to 253 whose digits add up to 7. Write every number with three digits (7 is 007). The first digit can be 0, 1 or 2. If it is 0 or 1, the number is already below 253 whatever comes next, so the last two digits are free: you just need \"two digits with a given sum\", a question that has nothing to do with 253 any more. Only the branch with first digit 2 stays on the edge, where the next digit is capped at 5. So the work is one narrow path along N's digits, with free sub-counts peeling off at each step, and those free sub-counts repeat endlessly across different prefixes. That repetition is what memoisation removes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The state\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>go(pos, total, tight)\u003c/code> counts the ways to fill positions pos onwards so that the final digit sum equals the target, given the sum so far and whether the prefix is still equal to N's.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>limit = digits[pos] if tight else 9\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For each digit d from 0 to limit, recurse into \u003ccode>go(pos + 1, total + d, tight and d == limit)\u003c/code>: the prefix stays tight only if it copied N's digit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>At the end (pos equals the number of digits), count 1 if the total matches, else 0. Prune early when the total already exceeds the target.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The summary is whatever the property needs and nothing more: \u003ccode>total % D\u003c/code> for \"digit sum divisible by D\", the last digit for \"no two equal neighbours\", a 10-bit mask for \"all digits distinct\", a count of 7s for \"exactly k sevens\".\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Leading zeros\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Writing short numbers with leading zeros (7 as 007) is harmless for sums and remainders, because zeros add nothing. It is wrong for properties that look at the digits themselves: with \"all digits distinct\", 007 would use the digit 0 twice. Add a fourth flag, \u003cb>started\u003c/b>: while it is false, a 0 is padding, does not enter the summary, and keeps started false; the first non-zero digit sets it. The number 0 itself (never started) is then handled explicitly.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Ranges, big N and counting from 1\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The count over [L, R] is \u003ccode>f(R) - f(L - 1)\u003c/code>, with f(-1) = 0. If the problem counts from 1, subtract the number 0 when it qualifies. When N has thousands of digits, pass it as a string, reduce counts modulo the requested prime, and fill a table position by position instead of recursing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are L positions (the number of digits), S summary values and 2 tight values, and each state tries at most 10 digits: \u003cb>O(L · S · 10) time, O(L · S) memory\u003c/b>. For N = 10^18 and digit sums up to 162, that is about 19 × 163 × 2 × 10, roughly 62,000 steps, against 10^18 for enumerating every number. The tight states form a single chain (one per position), so almost all the work and almost all the sharing happens in the free states. With a 10-bit used-digits mask, S is 1024: still only tens of thousands of states.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cues: \"how many integers in [L, R]\" or \"up to N\" with N as large as 10^18 or given as a long string, and a property checked digit by digit with a small summary. Not when the property needs the whole number at once with no small summary (whether x is prime), though \"x mod 7\" is fine because a remainder updates digit by digit (\u003ccode>r = (10 r + d) % 7\u003c/code>). For very simple properties a closed formula may be shorter, but digit DP is the method that generalises.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count x in [0, 253] with digit sum 7.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Position 0, tight: N's first digit is 2, so the first digit can be 0, 1 or 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>N        2  5  3\nprefix   _  _  _      sum 0   tight\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First digit 0 or 1: the prefix is now below N, so two \u003cb>free\u003c/b> digits remain. Two digits summing to 7: 8 ways (07, 16, ..., 70); summing to 6: 7 ways. These are memoised states go(1, 0, free) and go(1, 1, free)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix   0  _  _   -&gt;  \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\nprefix   1  _  _   -&gt;  \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First digit 2: still tight, sum 2, so the second digit is capped at N's 5. Digits 0 to 4 make the prefix smaller: one free digit must bring the sum to 7, exactly one way each (205, 214, 223, 232, 241)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix   2  0..4  _  -&gt;  \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second digit 5: still tight, sum 7, last digit capped at 3. Only 0 keeps the sum at 7 (the number 250); 1 to 3 overshoot and are pruned\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix   2  5  0     -&gt;  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nprefix   2  5  1..3  -&gt;  0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add up the branches: 8 + 7 + 5 + 1 = 21 numbers. Without the tight flag every three-digit string would count, giving 36\u003c/p>\u003cpre>total    \u003cmark>21\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from functools import cache\n\n\ndef count_digit_sum(n, target):\n    \"\"\"How many integers x in [0, n] have digit sum equal to target.\"\"\"\n    digits = [int(c) for c in str(n)]\n\n    @cache\n    def go(pos, total, tight):\n        if total &gt; target:\n            return 0\n        if pos == len(digits):\n            return 1 if total == target else 0\n        limit = digits[pos] if tight else 9\n        return sum(go(pos + 1, total + d, tight and d == limit) for d in range(limit + 1))\n\n    return go(0, 0, True)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The cache lives inside the call, so each n gets a fresh one. To adapt it, replace \u003ccode>total\u003c/code> with the summary your property needs, change the final check, and add \u003ccode>started\u003c/code> if leading zeros matter.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>No tight flag.\u003c/b> Counting every string of N's length counts numbers above N: 36 instead of 21 in the trace.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Leading zeros as real digits.\u003c/b> Properties about which digits appear need the \u003ccode>started\u003c/code> flag.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Off by one on ranges.\u003c/b> Use f(R) - f(L - 1), define f(-1) = 0, and check whether 0 itself should count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deep recursion.\u003c/b> The recursion is as deep as N has digits; Python fails somewhere past about 1,000 digits (the template raised RecursionError at 1,500). For long inputs, fill a table from the last position backwards.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Huge counts.\u003c/b> With thousands of digits the counts have thousands of digits too; reduce modulo the requested prime at every addition.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>count_digit_sum(99, 7)\u003c/code> return, and which numbers are they?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>8: 7, 16, 25, 34, 43, 52, 61 and 70. Both digits of 99 are 9s, so the tight cap never bites, and the count is just \"two digits summing to 7\".\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does memoisation help so much here, when the tight path visits each position only once?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The tight path is a single chain, but every step off it creates a free state (position, sum), and there are only L × S of those. Every prefix that drops below N with the same position and sum asks the same question, so its answer is computed once and reused, instead of enumerating all suffixes again.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you count the numbers in [1, N] with no two equal adjacent digits?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>State (pos, last digit, tight, started). Digit d is allowed if the number has not started yet or d differs from the last digit. Leading zeros keep started false and do not set the last digit; at the end count 1 only if started is true (this drops 0, as required for a range starting at 1).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>902 Numbers At Most N Given Digit Set.\u003c/b> Shorter lengths are all free; for N's length, walk N's digits counting smaller allowed digits times the free choices, and stop if N's digit itself is not allowed. Brute force: test every x up to N for small N. Expected: O(L · |digits|); \u003ccode>digits = [\"2\",\"5\",\"7\"], n = 300\u003c/code> gives 21, \u003ccode>digits = [\"1\",\"4\"], n = 41\u003c/code> gives 5, and \u003ccode>digits = [\"3\"], n = 2\u003c/code> gives 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>AtCoder DP S, Digit Sum.\u003c/b> Count x in [1, K] whose digit sum is divisible by D, modulo 10^9 + 7, with K up to 10^10000: state (pos, sum mod D, tight), filled as a table, then subtract the number 0. Expected: O(len(K) · D · 10); K = 50, D = 5 gives 10, K = 1000, D = 7 gives 139, and K = 9, D = 10 gives 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2376 Count Special Integers\u003c/b> (stretch). Distinct digits: state (pos, used-digits mask, tight, started). Expected: O(L · 2^10 · 10); n = 30 gives 28 (11 and 22 are excluded), n = 100 gives 90, n = 1000 gives 738.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count numbers up to N with a digit property using a tight flag, a started flag and a small summary state.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:dpadv/how-do-you-count-ways-modulo-10-9-7-without-gett":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Counting DPs produce astronomically large numbers, so problems ask for the count modulo a prime p, usually 10^9 + 7. Addition, subtraction and multiplication survive reduction, so reduce after every step. \u003cb>Subtraction\u003c/b> must end non-negative (automatic with Python's \u003ccode>%\u003c/code>, add p in C++). \u003cb>Division\u003c/b> becomes multiplication by an inverse, \u003ccode>pow(b, p - 2, p)\u003c/code>, which exists because p is prime. What you may never do is \u003cb>compare\u003c/b> reduced values: min, max and \"which is larger\" lose their meaning after a mod.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Clock arithmetic: ten hours after 9 o'clock is 7 o'clock, and adding or multiplying hours works perfectly on the clock face. But the clock face cannot tell you whether 7 o'clock is earlier or later than 9 o'clock, because it forgot how many times the hand went round. Counting modulo p is the same: every sum and product of the true counts can be tracked exactly \"on the clock\", while comparisons cannot.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why 10^9 + 7? It is prime, so every non-zero value has an inverse. It fits in a signed 32-bit integer, the sum of two residues is below 2^31, and the product of two residues is below 2^60, which fits in a signed 64-bit integer before reduction. (998244353, also prime, is the other common choice.)\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The rules\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Operation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Python\u003c/th>\u003cth>C++\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>a + b\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>(a + b) % p\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>(a + b) % p\u003c/code> (fits in 32 bits)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>a - b\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>(a - b) % p\u003c/code> (never negative in Python)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>((a - b) % p + p) % p\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>a × b\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a * b % p\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>(long long)a * b % p\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>a / b\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a * pow(b, p - 2, p) % p\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>pow(b, -1, p)\u003c/code> (Python 3.8+)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>fast power for the inverse\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>a &lt; b, min, max\u003c/td>\u003ctd>meaningless on reduced values\u003c/td>\u003ctd>meaningless\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Python's integers never overflow, so you could count exactly and reduce once at the end. It is correct and painfully slow: the exact number of ways to climb 10^5 stairs with steps of 1 to 3 has about 26,000 digits. Computing all of them exactly took 1.45 s and about 590 MB; reducing at every step took 0.04 s.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Subtraction: sliding sums\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The ways to climb i stairs with steps of 1 to k are the sum of the previous k values. Keeping a running window sum costs O(1) per step: add the newest value, subtract the one that falls out. That subtraction is where C++ code goes negative.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Division: binomials by inverse factorials\u003c/h5>\u003cp>C(n, r) = n! / (r! (n - r)!). Precompute factorials modulo p, take one inverse with Fermat's little theorem (\u003ccode>pow(fact[n], p - 2, p)\u003c/code>), and walk it down with \u003ccode>inv_fact[i - 1] = inv_fact[i] · i\u003c/code>. After O(n) setup, every C(n, r) costs three multiplications.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>What not to reduce\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Optimisation values (fewest coins, longest path) are never reduced: they are small anyway, and reducing breaks every min and max. In mixed DPs, such as \"the number of cheapest routes\", keep the cost exact and reduce only the count. And a reduced count of 0 does not prove that nothing is possible (the true count may be a multiple of p): track feasibility separately when it matters.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each reduced operation works on machine-sized numbers: O(1). A modular inverse by fast power costs O(log p), about 30 squarings for 10^9 + 7. Factorial tables cost O(n) time and space once. Unreduced big integers cost time proportional to their length per addition, so an n-step counting DP with exponential growth costs O(n²) digit operations instead of O(n).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Whenever a statement says \"return it modulo 10^9 + 7\": counting paths, matchings, arrangements, subsets, digit strings. Whenever a formula divides (combinations, expected values written as fractions modulo p). Never on the values being minimised or maximised.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ways to climb with steps of 1, 2 or 3, using a running window sum and a tiny modulus, p = 7, so that wrap-around is visible. The true counts are 1, 1, 2, 4, 7, 13, 24, 44.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1 to 3: the window just accumulates (nothing has fallen out yet)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0  1  2  3\nways     1  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\nwindow   after i = 3: 1 + 1 + 2 = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4: add ways[3] = 4, giving 8 % 7 = 1; subtract ways[0] = 1, giving 0. True count 7, and 7 % 7 = 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0  1  2  3  4\nways     1  1  2  4  \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5: add ways[4] = 0, giving 0; subtract ways[1] = 1, giving \u003cb>-1\u003c/b>. Python's -1 % 7 is 6; in C++ -1 % 7 is -1, a wrong answer. True count 13, and 13 % 7 = 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0  1  2  3  4  5\nways     1  1  2  4  0  \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 6: add 6, giving 12 % 7 = 5; subtract ways[2] = 2, giving 3. True count 24, and 24 % 7 = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0  1  2  3  4  5  6\nways     1  1  2  4  0  6  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 7: add 3, giving 6; subtract ways[3] = 4, giving 2. True count 44, and 44 % 7 = 2: every residue matches\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0  1  2  3  4  5  6  7\nways     1  1  2  4  0  6  3  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>MOD = 10**9 + 7\n\n\ndef ways_to_climb(n, k):\n    \"\"\"Ways to climb n steps taking 1..k steps at a time, mod MOD, in O(n).\"\"\"\n    ways = [0] * (n + 1)\n    ways[0] = 1\n    window = 0                                   # ways[i-k] + ... + ways[i-1], mod MOD\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        window = (window + ways[i - 1]) % MOD\n        if i - k - 1 &gt;= 0:\n            window = (window - ways[i - k - 1]) % MOD    # Python's % is never negative\n        ways[i] = window\n    return ways[n]\n\n\ndef binomials(n_max):\n    \"\"\"comb(n, r) mod MOD for 0 &lt;= n &lt;= n_max, after O(n_max) precomputation.\"\"\"\n    fact = [1] * (n_max + 1)\n    for i in range(1, n_max + 1):\n        fact[i] = fact[i - 1] * i % MOD\n    inv_fact = [1] * (n_max + 1)\n    inv_fact[n_max] = pow(fact[n_max], MOD - 2, MOD)     # Fermat: a^(p-2) is 1/a mod p\n    for i in range(n_max, 0, -1):\n        inv_fact[i - 1] = inv_fact[i] * i % MOD\n\n    def comb(n, r):\n        if r &lt; 0 or r &gt; n:\n            return 0\n        return fact[n] * inv_fact[r] % MOD * inv_fact[n - r] % MOD\n\n    return comb\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Test any modular DP against the exact count: on small inputs, Python computes the true big-integer answer for free, so \u003ccode>exact % MOD == fast\u003c/code> is a perfect oracle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Negative remainders in C++ and Java\u003c/b>, where \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> keeps the sign of the dividend: add p before the final \u003ccode>%\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflow in C++\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>a * b\u003c/code> with two \u003ccode>int\u003c/code> residues overflows before the \u003ccode>%\u003c/code>; cast to \u003ccode>long long\u003c/code> first. \u003ccode>pow\u003c/code> in C++ is floating point: write your own fast power.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Dividing with \u003ccode>/\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>//\u003c/code>.\u003c/b> Neither is modular division; multiply by the inverse.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No inverse.\u003c/b> 0 has no inverse, and neither does any multiple of p: factorial tables only work for n &lt; p.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reducing optimisation values\u003c/b> or comparing reduced counts.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Continue the trace to i = 8. What is \u003ccode>ways[8]\u003c/code> modulo 7, and what is the true count?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Add \u003ccode>ways[7] = 2\u003c/code> to the window 2, giving 4; subtract \u003ccode>ways[4] = 0\u003c/code>, giving 4. The true count is 13 + 24 + 44 = 81, and 81 % 7 = 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A DP stores \"the fewest coins\" and someone reduces it modulo 10^9 + 7 \"to be safe\". Why is that a bug even though the values are small?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It only stays correct as long as no value reaches p; if an intermediate value were ever reduced, min would compare residues instead of true values (5 against 10^9 + 8, which reduces to 1, would pick the wrong one). More importantly, it signals a misunderstanding: reduction is for counts that are only ever added and multiplied, never for values that are compared.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you compute C(10^5, 5·10^4) modulo 10^9 + 7, and what is it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Build \u003ccode>binomials(10**5)\u003c/code> once and call \u003ccode>comb(10**5, 5 * 10**4)\u003c/code>. It returns 149033233, which matches \u003ccode>math.comb(10**5, 5 * 10**4) % MOD\u003c/code> computed exactly with big integers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>AtCoder DP O, Matching\u003c/b> (counting part). Sum the bitmask DP counts with a reduction at every addition. Expected: \u003ccode>[[1,1,0],[1,1,1],[0,1,1]]\u003c/code> gives 3, and the all-ones 21 × 21 matrix gives 21! mod 10^9 + 7 = 72847302.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>AtCoder DP S, Digit Sum\u003c/b> (modular part). The DP counts 0 as well, so the answer is \u003ccode>(count - 1) % MOD\u003c/code>, which must stay correct when count is a multiple of p. Expected: K = 1000, D = 7 gives 139, and K = 9, D = 10 gives 0 (only 0 qualifies, and it is removed).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count modulo a prime safely: reduce sums and products, keep subtractions non-negative, divide with inverses, and never reduce what you compare.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:dpadv/how-do-you-speed-up-a-dp-whose-transitions-scan-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Many DPs are correct but slow because each state loops over a range of earlier states: \u003ccode>dp[i] = combine(dp[j] for j in a range)\u003c/code>. When the range moves predictably, maintain the combination incrementally instead of recomputing it. \u003cb>Prefix sums\u003c/b> give a range sum in O(1); a \u003cb>monotonic deque\u003c/b> gives a sliding-window maximum or minimum in amortised O(1); \u003cb>binary search\u003c/b> finds the single earlier state that matters when states are sorted. The recurrence stays the same; the cost drops from O(n·k) or O(n²) to O(n) or O(n log n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The recurrence is the specification; the inner loop is just one way to evaluate it. A bank that reports \"total spending over the last 30 days\" every morning does not re-add 30 days of transactions: it adds today's and subtracts the day that dropped out. Each technique below is that idea for a different kind of combine: sum, maximum, or \"the latest compatible one\".\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Prefix sums over transitions\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Share exactly K sweets among n children, where child i may get between 0 and \u003ccode>caps[i]\u003c/code>. Processing children one by one, \u003ccode>new[j] = ways[j] + ways[j-1] + ... + ways[j-cap]\u003c/code>: a sum over a contiguous window of the previous row. Build that row's prefix sums once, and every \u003ccode>new[j]\u003c/code> is one subtraction: \u003ccode>prefix[j+1] - prefix[max(0, j-cap)]\u003c/code>. The work drops from O(n·K·cap) to O(n·K). AtCoder DP M (Candies) is exactly this problem.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>A monotonic deque for window maxima\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Choose a non-empty subsequence with maximum sum where consecutive chosen indices are at most k apart. \u003ccode>best[i] = vals[i] + max(0, max(best[i-k..i-1]))\u003c/code>. The window max is the same problem the monotonic stack module solved with a deque: keep indices whose \u003ccode>best\u003c/code> values decrease from front to back; drop the front when it leaves the window; before appending i, pop every back index whose value is not larger than \u003ccode>best[i]\u003c/code> (it is older and no better, so it can never be the maximum again). The front is always the window's maximum. LeetCode 1425 (Constrained Subsequence Sum) and 1696 (Jump Game VI), both listed in the monotonic stack module, have this shape.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Binary search inside the DP\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Weighted interval scheduling: jobs with start, end and profit; take a profitable set of non-overlapping jobs. Sort by end time; \u003ccode>best[i+1] = max(best[i], best[p] + profit_i)\u003c/code>, where p counts the jobs ending no later than job i starts. The ends are sorted, so \u003ccode>bisect_right\u003c/code> finds p in O(log n) instead of a scan. LeetCode 1235 (Maximum Profit in Job Scheduling), listed in the DP module, is this problem; the O(n log n) longest increasing subsequence uses the same idea.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Spotting which one applies\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Write the slow recurrence first and look at its inner loop. A sum over a contiguous range: prefix sums. A maximum or minimum over a window that slides forwards: a monotonic deque. One relevant earlier state located by a sorted key: binary search, or two pointers when the queries also move monotonically. Further optimisations (the convex hull trick, divide and conquer optimisation, Knuth's) exist for contest problems and are beyond this guide.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Transition shape\u003c/th>\u003cth>Naive\u003c/th>\u003cth>Faster\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>sum over a window of width k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n·k)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>one prefix array per row\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>max or min over the last k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n·k)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>each index enters and leaves the deque once\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>latest compatible state by a sorted key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n²)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code> per state\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Measured in CPython: the sweets DP with 30 children, K = 1,000 and caps up to 1,000 took 1.38 s naively and 0.07 s with prefix sums; the gap-limited subsequence with n = 10^4 and k = 1,000 took 0.40 s naively and 0.008 s with the deque. The gains grow with k.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When the plain DP is correct but too slow by a factor equal to the range length, for example n·k = 10^10 with n = k = 10^5. Keep the slow version: it is the oracle for testing the fast one. Not applicable when the range is not contiguous or does not move monotonically; \"sum of dp[j] over all j &lt; i with a[j] &lt; a[i]\" needs a Fenwick tree over values (the range queries module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The deque version on vals = [3, -5, -2, 4, -1, 2] with k = 2. The deque holds indices; their \u003ccode>best\u003c/code> values are shown beside them.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0, value 3: the window is empty, so best[0] = 3. Push 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best    3\ndeque   [0]      values [3]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1, value -5: front 0 is in the window, best = -5 + 3 = -2. -2 is smaller than 3, so push 1 behind it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best    3  \u003cmark>-2\u003c/mark>\ndeque   [0, 1]   values [3, -2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2, value -2: front 0 still in the window (2 - 2 = 0), best = -2 + 3 = 1. Pop index 1 (-2 is not larger than 1), push 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best    3  -2  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\ndeque   [0, 2]   values [3, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3, value 4: front 0 is out of the window (it needs index 1 or later), pop it. best = 4 + 1 = 5. Pop 2 (1 &lt;= 5), push 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best    3  -2  1  \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\ndeque   [3]      values [5]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4, value -1: best = -1 + 5 = 4, push 4. i = 5, value 2: best = 2 + 5 = 7; pop 4 and 3, push 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best    3  -2  1  5  4  \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>\ndeque   [5]      values [7]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Answer max(best) = 7: indices 0, 2, 3, 5, that is 3 - 2 + 4 + 2, with gaps of 2, 1 and 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer  \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from bisect import bisect_right\nfrom collections import deque\n\nMOD = 10**9 + 7\n\n\ndef share_sweets(caps, total):\n    \"\"\"Ways to hand out exactly total sweets when child i may get 0..caps[i], mod MOD.\n    Prefix sums turn each O(cap) transition into O(1): O(n * total) overall.\"\"\"\n    ways = [1] + [0] * total                     # ways[j] for the children so far\n    for cap in caps:\n        prefix = [0] * (total + 2)\n        for j in range(total + 1):\n            prefix[j + 1] = (prefix[j] + ways[j]) % MOD\n        ways = [(prefix[j + 1] - prefix[max(0, j - cap)]) % MOD for j in range(total + 1)]\n    return ways[total]\n\n\ndef best_with_gaps(vals, k):\n    \"\"\"Maximum sum of a non-empty subsequence whose consecutive chosen indices are at most k apart.\n    best[i] = vals[i] + max(0, max(best[i-k..i-1])); a decreasing deque gives that max in O(1).\"\"\"\n    best = [0] * len(vals)\n    dq = deque()                                 # indices, best[] decreasing from front to back\n    for i, v in enumerate(vals):\n        if dq and dq[0] &lt; i - k:\n            dq.popleft()                         # fell out of the window\n        best[i] = v + max(0, best[dq[0]] if dq else 0)\n        while dq and best[dq[-1]] &lt;= best[i]:\n            dq.pop()                             # dominated: older and no better\n        dq.append(i)\n    return max(best)\n\n\ndef max_profit_jobs(jobs):\n    \"\"\"jobs: (start, end, profit), a job may start when the previous ends. O(n log n).\"\"\"\n    jobs = sorted(jobs, key=lambda j: j[1])\n    ends = [e for _, e, _ in jobs]\n    best = [0] * (len(jobs) + 1)                 # best[i]: using only the first i jobs\n    for i, (s, e, p) in enumerate(jobs):\n        fits = bisect_right(ends, s, 0, i)       # jobs ending at or before s\n        best[i + 1] = max(best[i], best[fits] + p)\n    return best[-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>One element leaves the window per step, so a single \u003ccode>if\u003c/code> is enough to evict from the front; the \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> at the back may pop many, but each index is popped at most once overall.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prefix off-by-ones.\u003c/b> With \u003ccode>prefix[j+1] = prefix[j] + ways[j]\u003c/code>, the sum of \u003ccode>ways[a..b]\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>prefix[b+1] - prefix[a]\u003c/code>; clamp a at 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prefix sums modulo p.\u003c/b> The subtraction can go negative in C++: add p.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Values instead of indices in the deque.\u003c/b> You cannot tell when a value has left the window; store indices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evicting after reading.\u003c/b> Drop out-of-window indices before using the front.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>bisect_left\u003c/code> where \u003ccode>bisect_right\u003c/code> is needed.\u003c/b> A job may start exactly when another ends; the right boundary includes it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Optimising before testing.\u003c/b> Verify the slow DP on small cases, then compare fast against slow on random inputs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run the deque version on vals = [5, -1, -1, -1, 6] with k = 2. What is the answer, and which indices achieve it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>best = [5, 4, 4, 3, 10], so the answer is 10: indices 0, 2 and 4 (5 - 1 + 6), each gap exactly 2. At i = 4 the window holds indices 2 and 3 with best values 4 and 3, so the front gives 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The deque loop has a \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> inside a \u003ccode>for\u003c/code>. Why is the total cost still O(n)?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each index is appended exactly once and removed at most once (from the front or the back), so the total number of pops over the whole run is at most n. The \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> cannot pop more than was pushed, so all the iterations together cost O(n): an amortised bound.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which speed-up fits \u003ccode>dp[i] = cost[i] + min(dp[j] for j in range(i - k, i))\u003c/code>, and which fits \u003ccode>dp[i] = sum(dp[j] for j in range(i - k, i))\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The first is a sliding-window minimum: a monotonic deque with increasing values. The second is a sliding-window sum: prefix sums, or simply a running window total as in the modular counting card.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exercise: sweets.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>share_sweets([1, 2, 3], 4)\u003c/code> gives 5 and \u003ccode>share_sweets([3, 3, 3], 5)\u003c/code> gives 12. Write the O(n·K·cap) version and compare on 200 random cases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exercise: gaps.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>best_with_gaps([10, -2, -10, -5, 20], 2)\u003c/code> gives 23 (indices 0, 1, 3, 4). Time the naive and deque versions for n = 10^4 and k = 10, 100 and 1,000, and check that the speed-up grows roughly in proportion to k.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exercise: jobs.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>max_profit_jobs([(0, 2, 5), (1, 4, 8), (2, 6, 7), (4, 7, 4)])\u003c/code> gives 12, and replacing \u003ccode>bisect_right\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>bisect_left\u003c/code> gives a wrong answer on this input. Then solve LeetCode 1235 (listed in the DP module) the same way.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to speed up DP transitions with prefix sums, a monotonic deque or binary search, keeping the slow DP as the oracle.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:dpadv/why-is-a-dp-a-shortest-or-longest-path-in-a-dag-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Make every DP state a vertex and every transition an edge weighted by its cost. A DP never depends on itself, so the graph is a \u003cb>DAG\u003c/b>: a topological order is an evaluation order, the base case is the source, and when each state builds on one predecessor the answer is a \u003cb>shortest or longest path\u003c/b> (optimisation), a \u003cb>number of paths\u003c/b> (counting) or \u003cb>reachability\u003c/b> (feasibility). Memoised recursion is a DFS over this DAG. The view pays off three ways: reconstruction is following parent pointers, a state graph \u003cb>with cycles\u003c/b> still yields to Dijkstra or BFS, and when few states are \u003cb>reachable\u003c/b>, a graph search touches only those.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Picture the DP table as a road map: the cells are towns, each transition is a one-way road with a toll, and the recurrence \"the best of my predecessors plus the road from them\" is exactly the relaxation step of the weighted shortest paths module. Filling the table in order is driving through the towns in topological order. Once you see the map, two questions open up: what if the roads form loops, and what if most towns can never be reached from the start?\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The dictionary\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>DP\u003c/th>\u003cth>Graph\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>state (subproblem)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>vertex\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>transition with cost c\u003c/td>\u003ctd>edge with weight c\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>base case\u003c/td>\u003ctd>source vertex\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>evaluation order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>topological order\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>min or max recurrence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>shortest or longest path\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>counting recurrence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>number of paths\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>memoised recursion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DFS with a visited cache, following edges backwards\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>reconstructing the answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>following parent pointers from the target\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Most DPs in this guide fit directly. Edit distance is a shortest path through the grid of (i, j) states, moving right, down or diagonally; Myers' algorithm, behind Git's default diff, searches that same edit graph. 0/1 knapsack is a longest path through (item, capacity used) states. Held-Karp is a shortest path through (mask, last) states. The jieba word segmenter makes sentence positions the vertices and candidate words the edges, weighted by -log probability, so the most probable segmentation is a shortest path.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Interval and tree DPs are the one twist: a state such as \u003ccode>dp[i][j]\u003c/code> combines \u003cb>two\u003c/b> smaller states (the two halves of a split), so its solution is a tree of subproblems rather than a single path (a hyperpath, in the jargon). The dependency graph is still a DAG and still fixes the evaluation order; only the \"answer is one path\" reading needs that adjustment.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>When the state graph has cycles\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Some problems have transitions that can return to an earlier state: walking on a grid in all four directions, toggling switches back and forth, a robot that can undo a move. Then no topological order exists, \"fill the table in order\" has no order to follow, and memoised recursion loops forever. The graph view tells you what to do instead: with non-negative costs run \u003cb>Dijkstra over the states\u003c/b>, with unit costs run \u003cb>BFS\u003c/b>. That is exactly how the paths module handled (cell, keys held) and (vertex, coupon used): DP states, graph algorithms.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>When most states are unreachable\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A table fills every state whether or not it matters. A search from the start touches only states that can actually occur. Take \"the fewest operations to turn n into 1, where an operation subtracts 1, halves an even number or divides a multiple of 3 by 3\". A bottom-up table needs an entry for every number up to n. A BFS from n reached 1 after visiting only 2,840 numbers when n = 10^9: the numbers that matter are those near n divided by small powers of 2 and 3.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A DP costs O(states + transitions) when each transition is O(1), which is the cost of one pass over its DAG; building no explicit graph is the usual implementation. Swapping the table for BFS costs the same order on the reachable part only, with hashing overhead per state (a dictionary instead of a list). Swapping it for Dijkstra adds a log factor: O((S + T) log S). Counting paths stays linear in the DAG's size but needs care with modular arithmetic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use the DAG view when you design a DP (draw a few states and their edges before writing a recurrence), when you need the actual solution rather than its value (store parents), when the \"DP\" has transitions that can loop (switch to Dijkstra or BFS), and when the state space is huge but sparse (search from the start). Stay with a plain table when every state is reachable and the order is obvious: arrays beat dictionaries by a large constant factor in Python.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Fewest operations from 10 to 1 (subtract 1, halve if even, divide by 3 if divisible), as BFS over states, levels in order.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start at 10 with distance 0. Its successors are 9 (subtract 1) and 5 (halve)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pop      10 (d=0)\nnew      9, 5\nqueue    [9, 5]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 9 (d=1): successors 8 (subtract 1) and 3 (divide by 3)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pop      9 (d=1)\nnew      8, \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\nqueue    [5, 8, 3]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 5 (d=1): only 4. Pop 8 (d=2): 7 (its half, 4, is already known)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pop      5, then 8\nnew      4, 7\nqueue    [3, 4, 7]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 3 (d=2): successors 2 and 1. The first time 1 is discovered, its distance is 3: 10, 9, 3, 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pop      3 (d=2)\nnew      2, \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark> (d=3)\nqueue    [4, 7, 2, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">BFS pops 1 at distance 3 and stops. Ten states touched; a table would have filled all of 1..10, and at n = 10^9 the search touches 2,840 states instead of a billion\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   (10 -&gt; 9 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 1)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\n\ndef fewest_steps_to_one(n):\n    \"\"\"Fewest operations (subtract 1, halve if even, third if divisible by 3) to turn n into 1.\n    Breadth-first search over the DAG of numbers: states are numbers, edges are operations.\"\"\"\n    dist = {n: 0}\n    queue = deque([n])\n    while queue:\n        x = queue.popleft()\n        if x == 1:\n            return dist[x]\n        for y in (x - 1, x // 2 if x % 2 == 0 else 0, x // 3 if x % 3 == 0 else 0):\n            if y &gt;= 1 and y not in dist:\n                dist[y] = dist[x] + 1\n                queue.append(y)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same skeleton becomes Dijkstra by swapping the deque for a heap of (cost, state), and becomes a counted-paths DP by processing states in topological order and adding counts along edges.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Memoised recursion on a cyclic state graph\u003c/b> never terminates (or returns a value read from a half-finished state). If transitions can return to a state, use BFS or Dijkstra.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Dictionaries everywhere.\u003c/b> For dense state spaces, arrays indexed by an encoded state are several times faster in Python.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reachability is not optimality.\u003c/b> BFS gives fewest steps only with unit costs; with varying costs use Dijkstra.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Huge sparse spaces need a target.\u003c/b> Searching from the start without an end condition explores everything reachable, which can still be enormous.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the trace, 2 is discovered at distance 3 and also has 1 as a successor. Why does 1 keep distance 3?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>BFS processes states level by level, so 1 was discovered from 3 at distance 3 before 2 was ever popped. A route through 2 would give 1 a distance of 4, and BFS ignores states already in \u003ccode>dist\u003c/code>, because with unit costs a later discovery can never be shorter.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can't you fill a table for \"fewest moves for a knight to reach each square of a chessboard\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Knight moves go back and forth, so the state graph has cycles and no topological order exists: a square's value depends on squares that depend on it. BFS from the start square computes the same quantities in distance order, which is the order that a cyclic graph with unit costs allows.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You need not just the minimum edit distance but the actual edits. What does the DAG view tell you to store?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A parent pointer per state, the predecessor (left, up or diagonal) that gave the minimum. Walking the pointers back from (m, n) to (0, 0) lists the edits in reverse, exactly like rebuilding a route from Dijkstra's parent array.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exercise: steps to one.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>fewest_steps_to_one(10)\u003c/code> gives 3 and \u003ccode>fewest_steps_to_one(10**9)\u003c/code> gives 30 while storing only a few thousand states. Write the bottom-up table version, check that both agree for every n up to 3,000, and compare their memory at n = 10^7.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exercise: a DP as a graph.\u003c/b> Draw the state DAG of the four-city TSP trace in the bitmask card: vertices are (set, end) pairs, edges add one city and weigh its distance. Expected: 13 vertices from ({0}, 0) to the three full-set states, and the optimal tour is the path ({0}, 0), ({0,2}, 2), ({0,2,3}, 3), ({0,1,2,3}, 1) plus the closing edge back to 0: 15 + 30 + 25 + 10 = 80.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to read any DP as a path problem in a DAG, rebuild solutions from parents, and switch to BFS or Dijkstra when states loop or are sparse.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","p:486":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two opponents share a row of integer scores. On each turn the current player removes either the leftmost or rightmost remaining score and adds it to their own total. Both play optimally for their own final score. Return whether the first player can finish with at least the second player's total; a tie counts as success. For \u003ccode>[1, 5, 2]\u003c/code>, the first player cannot secure a tie.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The row contains 1 to 20 nonnegative integers, each at most ten million. Picking from an end leaves a contiguous interval, so there are only quadratically many distinct positions despite exponentially many possible move sequences. Scores can be large, so reason about totals, not merely which player takes the larger number on one turn. Python integers avoid overflow.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two players alternately take a number from either end of a row; can the first player finish with at least as much as the second?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Both players always face the same kind of position, a contiguous stretch of the row. What single number about a stretch would let both use one formula?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Interval DP\u003c/p>\u003cp>Let dp[i][j] be the best score difference the player to move can force on nums[i..j]: max(nums[i] - dp[i+1][j], nums[j] - dp[i][j-1]). Fill by increasing length; player 1 wins when dp[0][n-1] &gt;= 0.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n²) time, O(n) space with one row\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Explore both legal end choices until the row is empty, alternating which total receives the chosen value. A minimax search returns the best outcome under optimal opposition. Its branching can create O(2^n) move sequences and O(n) recursion depth. Many sequences reach the same interval; caching or tabulation removes that repeated work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Define the value of an interval as the largest final score difference, current player minus other player, that its player to move can force. If they take the left value, the other player becomes the current player on the remaining interval, whose optimal difference is subtracted. The right choice is symmetric. Each choice really is achievable, and the opponent can enforce the stated remainder, so the larger of the two differences is exactly the minimax value. A singleton has value equal to its number.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For fixed left endpoint, build right endpoints upward. Before updating \u003ccode>dp[j]\u003c/code>, it holds the value for the interval starting one position later; \u003ccode>dp[j-1]\u003c/code> has already been updated for the current left endpoint. Thus the original two-dimensional recurrence needs only one array.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On \u003ccode>[1, 5, 2]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Singletons\u003c/p>\u003cpre>dp = [1, 5, 2].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Left endpoint 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Interval [5, 2] has value max(5 - 2, 2 - 5) = 3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Left endpoint 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Interval [1, 5] has value 4. The whole row has value max(1 - 3, 2 - 4) = -2, so the answer is false.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def first_player_can_tie(nums):\n    dp = nums[:]\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 2, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(nums)):\n            dp[j] = max(nums[i] - dp[j], nums[j] - dp[j - 1])\n    return dp[-1] &gt;= 0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(n²) interval updates, each constant time. The copied array uses O(n) auxiliary space and leaves the input unchanged. The nonempty-row constraint ensures that the final entry exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Subtract the opponent's optimal difference rather than adding it; the roles have switched. Iterate right endpoints from left to right, because the opposite order reads an old state for the shorter interval. A singleton is always won under the stated nonnegative constraints, and zero differences must return true. A greedy choice of the bigger endpoint can sacrifice a better future reply.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Retain the full interval table and record decisions to reconstruct optimal moves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the maximum score difference instead of converting it to a Boolean result.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:337":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node of a binary tree contains a nonnegative amount. Choose nodes with maximum total value, subject to never choosing both ends of a parent-child edge. Return that maximum. For a root worth 3 with children worth 2 and 4, skipping the root yields 6. The supplied node objects expose \u003ccode>val\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There can be up to 10,000 nodes, with values from zero to 10,000. The tree may be a long chain, so a recursive implementation could exceed Python's recursion limit. A child subtree interacts with the rest of the tree only through whether its root is selected. An empty tree is supported here and contributes zero, even though standard inputs are nonempty.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Houses form a binary tree, and two directly linked houses cannot both be robbed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A node's best total depends on whether the node itself is robbed. How many numbers should each subtree report upwards?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tree DP and rerooting\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each subtree returns a pair (rob, skip): rob = val + left.skip + right.skip and skip = max(left) + max(right), computed children-first; the answer is the larger number of the root's pair.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all subsets of the nodes, discard any subset containing an adjacent pair, and maximize the sum. This takes O(n 2^n) time with a straightforward adjacency check and O(n) working space. It ignores that once a parent's choice is fixed, separate child subtrees can be optimized independently.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each subtree calculate two totals: the best if its root is selected and the best if it is skipped. Selecting a root forces both children to be skipped, while skipping it permits the better of each child's two choices. These are exhaustive and independently achievable because the child subtrees have no cross edges. Induction from empty children therefore proves both totals optimal.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use an explicit stack with an enter/exit marker to obtain postorder without recursive calls. Store child pairs until their parent is processed, and remove them when consumed. The null child contributes \u003ccode>(0, 0)\u003c/code>. The root answer is the larger of its two totals.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For a root of value 3 and two leaf children 2 and 4:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Leaves\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The left child reports (take 2, skip 0); the right reports (take 4, skip 0).\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Root\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Taking it gives 3 + 0 + 0 = 3. Skipping it gives 2 + 4 = 6.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Result\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The maximum of the two root states is 6.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def max_tree_robbery(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return 0\n    stack = [(root, False)]\n    values = {}\n    while stack:\n        node, ready = stack.pop()\n        if not ready:\n            stack.append((node, True))\n            if node.right is not None:\n                stack.append((node.right, False))\n            if node.left is not None:\n                stack.append((node.left, False))\n        else:\n            left = values.pop(id(node.left), (0, 0))\n            right = values.pop(id(node.right), (0, 0))\n            values[id(node)] = (node.val + left[1] + right[1],\n                                max(left) + max(right))\n    return max(values[id(root)])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is entered and exited once, so time is O(n). The explicit postorder stack and the retained sibling results occupy O(h) space for tree height h, including a chain's O(n) worst case. Node identities, not values, distinguish equal-valued nodes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A parent may be skipped even if it is more valuable than either child individually; compare the combined subtree outcomes. Grandchildren may be chosen together with their grandparent. A zero-valued leaf is harmless. Avoid keying states by node value and avoid relying on recursion for a deep tree. The input must be a tree, not a graph with shared children.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep take/skip decisions to recover a set of selected nodes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generalize from two children to an arbitrary number of children using sums over their states.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1986":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a list of indivisible task durations and a fixed session capacity, schedule every task into as few sessions as possible. Tasks may be reordered, but each must fit wholly within one session. Return the number of sessions. For durations \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code> and capacity 3, two sessions suffice: one with 3 and one with 1 and 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 14 tasks. Durations are positive and individually no larger than the session capacity, which is at most 15. A task subset can therefore be encoded in 14 bits. The sum divided by capacity gives only a lower bound: indivisible durations can prevent an exact packing. This implementation also accepts an empty list and returns zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At most 14 tasks must be packed into the fewest work sessions of a fixed length.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With so few tasks, the set of finished tasks can be the whole state. What else do you need to know to place the next task?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Bitmask DP over subsets\u003c/p>\u003cp>dp[mask] = (sessions used, time used in the current session), minimised as a pair; adding task i either fits in the current session or opens a new one. Masks in increasing order are a valid evaluation order.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(2^n · n) time, O(2^n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assign each task to a labeled session and try all feasible assignments, or enumerate task orders and possible session breaks. The number of possibilities grows exponentially or worse, and many different assignments represent the same set of completed tasks. A mask avoids revisiting equivalent subsets while keeping just enough information about the last session.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each mask store the lexicographically smallest pair \u003ccode>(sessions, load)\u003c/code> among schedules of those tasks, where load is the time used in the most recently opened session. Appending a task either fits there or starts another session. A smaller pair dominates a larger one for all future append operations: if their session counts agree, a smaller load fits at least as much; if one uses fewer sessions, it can open an extra session before falling behind, and when counts become equal its current load is no greater. Thus discarding the larger pair cannot lose an optimal completion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every packing can be ordered by listing its sessions and then the tasks within each session. Consequently appending tasks covers an optimal packing. Adding a bit always increases the mask, so increasing numeric order evaluates predecessors first. Seed the empty mask with zero sessions and zero load; the first task explicitly opens a session.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For capacity 3 and tasks \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Empty\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The state is (0, 0); adding task 1 opens a session and produces (1, 1).\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Combine\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Adding task 2 to that mask fits, producing (1, 3).\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Finish\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Task 3 opens a second session, giving (2, 3). The alternative order with task 3 first also uses two sessions, so the answer is 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def min_work_sessions(tasks, capacity):\n    n = len(tasks)\n    dp = [(n + 1, 0)] * (1 &lt;&lt; n)\n    dp[0] = (0, 0)\n    for mask in range(1 &lt;&lt; n):\n        sessions, load = dp[mask]\n        for i, duration in enumerate(tasks):\n            if mask &amp; (1 &lt;&lt; i):\n                continue\n            if sessions and load + duration &lt;= capacity:\n                candidate = (sessions, load + duration)\n            else:\n                candidate = (sessions + 1, duration)\n            nxt = mask | (1 &lt;&lt; i)\n            dp[nxt] = min(dp[nxt], candidate)\n    return dp[-1][0]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 2^n masks and at most n outgoing choices per mask: O(n 2^n) time and O(2^n) auxiliary space. The tuple comparison is constant time. The state represents the used load, not remaining capacity, so smaller is preferable on a tie.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not treat each task as splittable across sessions or assume the total-duration lower bound is attainable. Exact capacity fits in the current session. Repeated durations are still distinct tasks, represented by different bits. The code assumes every duration fits by itself; reject oversized tasks separately if a different interface permits them. An empty list requires no sessions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store a predecessor per mask to recover one optimal sequence of tasks and its session breaks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a small capacity but many tasks, investigate frequency-based packing rather than a mask over individual tasks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1547":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A stick runs from coordinate 0 to coordinate \u003ccode>length\u003c/code>. Every coordinate in a given list must be cut once. When a cut is made, its cost is the current length of the piece being divided. Choose an order of cuts with minimum total cost and return that cost. For length 7 and cut positions \u003ccode>[1, 3, 4, 5]\u003c/code>, an optimal total is 16. The coordinates refer to the original stick throughout; cutting does not renumber them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The length is at most 1,000,000, with 1 to 100 distinct integer cuts strictly inside the stick. Since cost depends on the surrounding already-made cuts, sorting by coordinate or always cutting the shortest current piece is not obviously optimal. There are far too many possible orders to enumerate, but every current piece is bounded by two positions in the sorted set of cuts plus the endpoints. This gives O(m²) possible intervals for m cut positions. The function also handles an empty cut list and returns zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Cut a stick at given points, each cut costing the length of the piece it splits; find the cheapest order.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Look at a segment between two neighbouring positions in the final set of cuts. What does the first cut made inside it leave behind?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Interval DP\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort the cuts and add 0 and n; for positions i &lt; j, dp[i][j] = c[j] - c[i] + min over k strictly between of dp[i][k] + dp[k][j]. The first cut inside a segment splits it into two independent segments.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m³) time, O(m²) space for m cuts\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try all m! permutations of the cuts. For each permutation simulate the current pieces or maintain sorted boundaries, and add the length of the piece containing the next position. This directly describes the objective but repeats the same segment choices in many orders. A simple simulation with linear boundary searches costs O(m · m!) time and O(m) space, excluding the input. It is useful only for tiny instances and serves as an independent check of a dynamic program.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort the coordinates and include the two endpoints. Consider the open interval between boundary indices \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>j\u003c/code>. If there are no required positions strictly inside it, its remaining cost is zero. Otherwise some interior position \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> must be the first cut in this piece. That first cut always costs the full boundary distance, regardless of where \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> lies. It produces two independent pieces; all subsequent cuts occur in one or the other. Their optimal costs can therefore be added.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Take the minimum over every legal first cut. Any valid cutting order has one such first cut and cannot beat the optimal costs of its two resulting pieces, giving a lower bound. Conversely, perform a minimizing first cut followed by optimal orders for both pieces. This achieves the bound. Induction over the number of interior coordinates proves the recurrence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Fill intervals by increasing index gap. Both child gaps are strictly smaller than the parent's, so their values are ready. Adjacent boundaries need no work. Copy before sorting so callers retain their original cut order. The algorithm optimizes total cost rather than producing the actual sequence; a recorded minimizing index would recover it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take length 7 with cuts \u003ccode>[1, 3]\u003c/code>, giving boundaries \u003ccode>[0, 1, 3, 7]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Base intervals\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Each pair of adjacent boundaries has cost 0 because it contains no cut.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">One internal cut\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The interval 0 to 3 costs 3, and the interval 1 to 7 costs 6.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Whole stick\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Cutting at 1 first costs 7 + 0 + 6 = 13. Cutting at 3 first costs 7 + 3 + 0 = 10. Return 10.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The first operation is charged once at its own enclosing piece. The child costs already include all later operations, so adding another copy of their boundary lengths would double count.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def min_cut_cost(length, cuts):\n    points = [0] + sorted(cuts) + [length]\n    size = len(points)\n    dp = [[0] * size for _ in range(size)]\n    for gap in range(2, size):\n        for left in range(size - gap):\n            right = left + gap\n            dp[left][right] = points[right] - points[left] + min(\n                dp[left][mid] + dp[mid][right]\n                for mid in range(left + 1, right)\n            )\n    return dp[0][-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(m²) boundary pairs, each considering up to m first cuts, for O(m³) time and O(m²) auxiliary space. Sorting costs O(m log m) and the copied boundary list uses O(m) space, both dominated for the intended sizes. The numerical stick length affects the values but not the number of states.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The cost is the current piece's length, not the distance from the cut to one side. Include 0 and the full length as permanent boundaries, and do not treat them as required cuts. Sort an unsorted input before constructing intervals. One cut costs the full stick length; no cuts cost zero. Duplicate or out-of-range positions violate the contract and should be validated separately if accepted by an application. Choosing a middle cut can help on some inputs but is not a correctness proof.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Save the best first cut per interval and traverse the resulting split tree to reconstruct an optimal order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If each cut has an additional fixed fee, add that fee for its selected position in each transition.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:312":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A row of balloons has nonnegative integer values. When one is removed, earn its value multiplied by the values of its two current neighbors. Beyond each end, an imaginary neighbor has value 1 and remains present. Continue until the row is empty and return the largest achievable total. For \u003ccode>[3, 1, 5, 8]\u003c/code>, the optimum is 167. Removing a balloon makes its former neighbors adjacent, so their future rewards may change.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The row contains at most 300 balloons, each valued from 0 to 100. The official input is nonempty; the implementation also gives zero for an empty row. There are factorially many removal orders, but the useful subproblems can be intervals bounded by balloons that are temporarily retained. Zero values are legal, and removing one can change neighboring products even when its own reward is zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Burst balloons in a row for coins equal to the product of a balloon and its current neighbours; maximise the total.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Choosing the first balloon to burst makes its neighbours touch and tangles the two sides. Which balloon in a stretch has neighbours you know for certain?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Interval DP\u003c/p>\u003cp>Pad with 1s and let dp[l][r] be the best for the balloons strictly between l and r. If k bursts last there, its neighbours are l and r, so dp[l][r] = max over k of dp[l][k] + a[l]·a[k]·a[r] + dp[k][r], filled by increasing gap.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n³) time, O(n²) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each current row, try removing each index, add its immediate reward, and recursively explore the remaining row. The total is the maximum across the resulting orders. There are n! possible orders and O(n) work per naive removal and copy, giving O(n · n!) time and O(n²) memory along a copying recursion stack. This simulation naturally handles the changing neighbors and is a valuable oracle for short rows, but it does not scale.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choosing the first removal in a segment is awkward: the two sides become neighbors and influence one another. Instead fix the last balloon \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> removed strictly between two retained boundaries \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>. By that time, every other interior balloon is gone, so its reward is exactly the product of those two boundary values and its own value. The earlier removals lie entirely in the two smaller open intervals. The boundaries remain present throughout, so those two subproblems can be optimized independently.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For any complete order, its final interior removal identifies one such \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>; its earlier rewards are bounded by the best values for the two subintervals. Conversely, run the two optimal suborders in either sequence while keeping the boundaries, then remove \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> last. This achieves their sum plus the final product. Taking the maximum over \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> proves the recurrence. Padding the row with ones handles the external neighbors without special transitions.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Initialize an interval with no interior balloon to zero and increase the gap between boundary indices. Both intervals around a candidate have smaller gaps, ensuring their answers have already been computed. The calculation does not mutate the supplied list. Retaining a maximizing index allows later reconstruction of an order: recursively perform the two interior orders before bursting the recorded last balloon.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[2, 3]\u003c/code>, padding yields \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 1]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Single-balloon intervals\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Removing 2 between boundaries 1 and 3 earns 6. Removing 3 between boundaries 2 and 1 earns 6.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Choose the last balloon\u003c/p>\u003cpre>If 2 is last, the total is 6 + 1*2*1 = 8. If 3 is last, it is 6 + 1*3*1 = 9.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Result\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Return 9, achieved by removing 2 first and 3 second.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The single-balloon subproblem containing 2 uses 3 as its right boundary because 3 has not yet been removed in that candidate order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def max_burst_coins(nums):\n    values = [1] + list(nums) + [1]\n    size = len(values)\n    dp = [[0] * size for _ in range(size)]\n    for gap in range(2, size):\n        for left in range(size - gap):\n            right = left + gap\n            dp[left][right] = max(\n                dp[left][k] + values[left] * values[k] * values[right]\n                + dp[k][right]\n                for k in range(left + 1, right)\n            )\n    return dp[0][-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>O(n²) open intervals each try O(n) final balloons, so time is O(n³). The table and padded values use O(n²) auxiliary space. The cost does not depend on the magnitudes except for integer arithmetic; Python's integers avoid fixed-width overflow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The boundary balloons are not removed inside the state; including them among candidates breaks the recurrence. Reward is based on current neighbors, not the original neighbors. A singleton earns its own value because both outside neighbors equal one. All-zero rows return zero, and an empty row has no reward. Do not assume the same recurrence works unchanged for negative values if using zero as a default for a nonempty interval; the code explicitly maximizes over real candidates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Record the last choice in every interval and output a maximizing removal order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace the outside ones with specified permanent boundary values and reuse the interval formulation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2246":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A rooted tree is described by a parent array and one character per node. A path is valid when every pair of consecutive nodes on it has different characters. Return the maximum number of nodes on any valid simple path; the path may pass through the root, remain inside one subtree, or contain just one node. For parents \u003ccode>[-1, 0, 0]\u003c/code> and labels \u003ccode>\"aba\"\u003c/code>, the best path has two nodes: node 0 and node 1.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 100,000 nodes, root 0 has parent -1, and the other parent entries describe a valid rooted tree. Labels are lowercase letters. Parent indices need not be smaller than their children's indices, so merely scanning indices backward is not necessarily a postorder. A chain can be as deep as the entire tree; an explicit traversal avoids Python recursion limits. Count nodes, not edges.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest path in a tree on which neighbouring nodes always carry different letters.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>As with a diameter, every path has a highest node. What should each node report upwards, and what does it combine at its own level?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tree DP and rerooting\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each node returns its longest valid downward chain; at node u, keep the two longest chains from children whose letter differs from u's and update the answer with 1 + top1 + top2. Process nodes children-first using the parent array, without recursion.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each pair of endpoints, find their unique tree path, check every adjacent label pair, and keep the longest valid path. Up to O(n²) pairs each require O(n) work with a simple path search, for O(n³) time and O(n) auxiliary space. This is a useful small-tree oracle. A single traversal can instead reuse the best partial path already computed below each node.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Root the tree as given. Any simple path has a highest node: its endpoints lie in at most two different child subtrees of that node, and the rest of the path descends from it. Let each node report its longest valid downward chain including itself. For a parent, only children with a different label can extend such a chain across the connecting edge. Among those, the largest child chain supplies the best downward report, while the two largest together supply the best path whose highest node is the parent.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This considers every possible highest node. For a fixed highest node, using more than two child branches would not form a simple path; using anything shorter than the best eligible chain in a chosen branch cannot improve its length. Conversely, joining the two eligible chains through the node gives a valid path. By induction from leaves, the downward reports and the maximum over all nodes are exact.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build children lists, then visit nodes from the root to obtain an order. Reversing that order ensures each child is processed before its parent regardless of numeric labels. Store the lengths in an array and retain the largest and second-largest eligible contributions at each node. A global maximum also catches paths that never pass through the root. A leaf has a chain length of one, and zero is a convenient absent-branch length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For parent array \u003ccode>[-1, 0, 0, 1]\u003c/code> and labels \u003ccode>\"abca\"\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Leaves\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Nodes 2 and 3 each report a downward chain of length 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Node 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Its label b differs from child 3's a, so it reports 2 and offers a two-node path.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Node 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Both children differ from a. Their contributions are 2 and 1, so a path through 0 has 1 + 2 + 1 = 4 nodes.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The global answer is 4, corresponding to nodes 3, 1, 0, and 2. The two branches are distinct because each child is examined exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_distinct_adjacent_path(parent, labels):\n    n = len(parent)\n    children = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for node in range(1, n):\n        children[parent[node]].append(node)\n    order = [0]\n    for node in order:\n        order.extend(children[node])\n    down = [1] * n\n    best = 1\n    for node in reversed(order):\n        first = second = 0\n        for child in children[node]:\n            if labels[child] == labels[node]:\n                continue\n            length = down[child]\n            if length &gt; first:\n                first, second = length, first\n            elif length &gt; second:\n                second = length\n        down[node] = 1 + first\n        best = max(best, 1 + first + second)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building children, collecting the traversal order, and processing each edge take O(n) time. Children lists, the traversal order, and downward lengths use O(n) auxiliary space. Reversing an existing list does not introduce recursive frames. The answer is at most n.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Comparing labels across a child edge is essential; a same-label child may still contain the best path deeper in its own subtree, so do not discard the entire subtree from processing. The two selected chains must come from different children. The global result may be a leaf, including when every label is identical. A one-node tree returns one, not zero. Parent-array indexing order is not a substitute for a real topological traversal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Retain the endpoints of the two best chains to reconstruct an actual longest path.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace unequal labels with any symmetric edge predicate; the same path-combination argument still applies.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:834":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every vertex of an undirected tree, compute the sum of its edge distances to all vertices, including itself. Return the sums in vertex-number order. In a three-node path with edges \u003ccode>0-1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>1-2\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[3, 2, 3]\u003c/code>: the middle has distance one to each endpoint, while an endpoint has distances one and two to the others.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 30,000 vertices numbered from zero and exactly n-1 edges forming a connected tree. Each edge has unit length. One search from each vertex is correct but quadratic, whereas moving the root across one edge changes all distances in a predictable way. A path-shaped tree can have depth n, so iterative passes avoid recursive stack limits. Python integer sums can exceed 32-bit range without overflowing.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For every node of a tree, the sum of its distances to all other nodes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Solve it for one root first. When the root moves across one edge, which distances grow by one and which shrink by one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tree DP and rerooting\u003c/p>\u003cp>One children-first pass computes subtree sizes and the root's total; moving the root from p to its child c gives ans[c] = ans[p] - size[c] + (n - size[c]), applied top-down from the root.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Run breadth-first search from every starting vertex, summing the distances discovered in that search. A tree has O(n) vertices and edges, so this costs O(n²) time and O(n) working space, besides the output. It is an excellent oracle for small random trees but repeats nearly identical work for adjacent starting vertices.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First choose node 0 as a temporary root. A traversal supplies each node's parent and distance from 0, so summing those distances gives the first answer. In reverse traversal order, add each child's subtree size to its parent, counting every vertex once. Now imagine shifting the root from a parent \u003ccode>p\u003c/code> to its child \u003ccode>c\u003c/code>. All \u003ccode>size[c]\u003c/code> vertices in c's subtree become one edge closer. Each of the remaining \u003ccode>n - size[c]\u003c/code> vertices becomes one edge farther. Therefore the new sum equals the old sum minus \u003ccode>size[c]\u003c/code> plus \u003ccode>n - size[c]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every vertex belongs to exactly one of those groups because removing edge \u003ccode>p-c\u003c/code> separates a tree into two components. This proves the update, not merely a heuristic. Processing children after parents propagates the root answer along the unique path from node 0 to each vertex. The original subtree sizes remain defined with respect to the temporary root; they are not recomputed after each conceptual move.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Construct a parent-before-child order iteratively. The reverse order computes sizes; the forward order computes the rerooted sums. The parent array doubles as a visited marker, with root assigned itself so it is never revisited through a neighbor. No distances between arbitrary pairs need to be stored.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The choice of initial root is arbitrary: it changes the intermediate sizes and first sum but not the requested output. Every later result follows by applying the same edge argument, so the method does not depend on vertex numbering or adjacency-list order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For the path \u003ccode>0-1-2\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Root at 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Depths are [0, 1, 2], so the root sum is 3. Subtree sizes are [3, 2, 1].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Move from 0 to 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Two vertices get closer and one gets farther: answer[1] = 3 - 2 + 1 = 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Move from 1 to 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>One vertex gets closer and two get farther: answer[2] = 2 - 1 + 2 = 3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The sizes in the last step are still those measured under root 0; the final array is \u003ccode>[3, 2, 3]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def tree_distance_sums(n, edges):\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for a, b in edges:\n        graph[a].append(b)\n        graph[b].append(a)\n    parent = [-1] * n\n    parent[0] = 0\n    depth = [0] * n\n    order = [0]\n    for node in order:\n        for neighbor in graph[node]:\n            if parent[neighbor] == -1:\n                parent[neighbor] = node\n                depth[neighbor] = depth[node] + 1\n                order.append(neighbor)\n    size = [1] * n\n    for node in reversed(order[1:]):\n        size[parent[node]] += size[node]\n    answer = [0] * n\n    answer[0] = sum(depth)\n    for node in order[1:]:\n        answer[node] = answer[parent[node]] + n - 2 * size[node]\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The adjacency list contains 2(n-1) entries. Both traversals and all array initialization take O(n) time, and graph storage plus the working arrays take O(n) auxiliary space. The returned n-element list itself also requires O(n) space. A root-only tree produces \u003ccode>[0]\u003c/code> without any edge transitions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Distances are measured in edges; the root's own distance is zero. Subtract the child's subtree size and add the complement, not the other way around. Use the same initial rooting when interpreting every size. Do not rely on the input edge orientation: these edges are undirected. The interface assumes a connected tree with n at least one; a disconnected graph or cycle would require separate validation and a different proof.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For positive edge weights, multiply the reroot adjustment by the weight of the crossed edge and use weighted root distances.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace a plain count of vertices with vertex weights to calculate weighted distance sums.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:ac:dp_o":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are equally many people on two sides of a matching. A Boolean square matrix says which pairs are allowed. Count the one-to-one assignments that use every person exactly once and use only allowed pairs. Return the count modulo 1,000,000,007. For \u003ccode>[[1, 1], [1, 1]]\u003c/code>, either assignment works, so the answer is 2. Matrix rows identify the first side and columns the second.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The matrix has size N by N with 1 &lt;= N &lt;= 21 and entries zero or one. N! assignments are too numerous, but a mask of the used columns has at most 2^21 possibilities. The number of used columns also determines how many rows have already been assigned, so no additional row dimension is needed. The implementation naturally returns one for an empty matrix: the empty assignment.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the ways to pair N men with N women (N at most 21) when only some pairs are compatible, modulo 10^9 + 7.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If the men are paired in a fixed order, the number of women already taken tells you which man comes next. So what is the state?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Bitmask DP over subsets\u003c/p>\u003cp>dp[mask] counts the ways to pair the first popcount(mask) men with exactly the women in mask; the next man can take any compatible woman j outside mask, adding dp[mask] into dp[mask | 1 &lt;&lt; j] modulo p.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(2^N · N) time, O(2^N) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all N! permutations of columns and test each row's chosen entry. This uses O(N · N!) time and O(N) space with a typical permutation generator. It is an independent way to verify small matrices, but most permutations share partial assignments that can be counted together.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let \u003ccode>dp[mask]\u003c/code> count assignments of the first \u003ccode>popcount(mask)\u003c/code> rows to exactly the columns in the mask. Initially the empty mask has one assignment. For a state with k bits, the next row is k; append any allowed column whose bit is not yet set. Adding the state's count to the new mask counts every extension.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each full assignment has exactly one final column for the last row, and deleting that assignment gives exactly one predecessor. Therefore the recurrence neither misses nor duplicates any assignment. Increasing numeric mask order works because setting an unset bit strictly increases the mask. Reduction after each addition preserves the final residue because addition respects modular equivalence. There is no modular division involved.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[[1, 1], [1, 1]]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Empty mask\u003c/p>\u003cpre>dp[00] = 1. Row 0 may select either column, producing dp[01] = dp[10] = 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">One selected column\u003c/p>\u003cpre>From 01, row 1 can select column 1; from 10, it can select column 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Complete mask\u003c/p>\u003cpre>dp[11] = 1 + 1 = 2, the answer.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_matchings(allowed):\n    mod = 1_000_000_007\n    n = len(allowed)\n    dp = [0] * (1 &lt;&lt; n)\n    dp[0] = 1\n    for mask in range(1 &lt;&lt; n):\n        row = mask.bit_count()\n        if row == n:\n            continue\n        for col in range(n):\n            if allowed[row][col] and not (mask &amp; (1 &lt;&lt; col)):\n                nxt = mask | (1 &lt;&lt; col)\n                dp[nxt] = (dp[nxt] + dp[mask]) % mod\n    return dp[-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 2^N masks and at most N column checks per mask, so time is O(N 2^N) and auxiliary space is O(2^N). Modular reduction bounds stored counts. Bit counting and masks are constant-size operations under the stated N &lt;= 21 bound.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each row must be used once; a compatible column already present in the mask cannot be selected again. An all-zero row forces the result to zero. A 1-by-1 matrix returns its sole entry. Do not iterate the next row after the full mask or reduce Boolean compatibility itself modulo the count. The algorithm assumes a square matrix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Track predecessors for a single valid assignment rather than its count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>To handle more columns than rows, stop when the mask has the number of bits equal to the number of rows and sum those states.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:902":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You are given a set of allowed nonzero decimal digits and a positive integer bound. Count the positive integers no greater than the bound whose decimal representation uses only digits from that set. Digits may be reused. For digits \u003ccode>['1', '3']\u003c/code> and bound 31, the valid numbers are 1, 3, 11, 13, and 31, so the answer is 5. The bound itself counts when all of its digits are permitted.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The digit set contains distinct characters from \u003ccode>'1'\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>'9'\u003c/code>, and the positive bound is at most 10^9. Excluding zero means every choice in a shorter representation is valid as a leading digit. A direct scan to the bound can be far larger than the number of decimal positions. Comparing equal-length decimal strings digit by digit captures numeric order because their lengths match.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the positive integers up to n that can be written using only digits from a given set.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Numbers shorter than n are unrestricted. For numbers as long as n, what happens once a digit is smaller than n's digit at the same place?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Digit DP with a tight flag\u003c/p>\u003cp>Add d^len for every shorter length. Then walk n's digits: at each place add (allowed digits smaller than n's digit) times d^(places left), continue only if n's digit itself is allowed, and add 1 if all of n's digits are allowed.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(L · d) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Visit each integer from 1 through the bound, convert it to decimal, and check every character against the set. This takes O(bound · L) time in the worst case for L digits and O(L) temporary space for the conversion. Alternatively, generate all allowed digit strings, but there can still be exponentially many in L. Both approaches are practical as checks only for small bounds.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Partition candidates by length. Every positive number with fewer digits than the bound is automatically smaller, and each of its positions has d independent choices, giving d^k candidates of length k. For numbers with the same length, scan the bound from left to right while maintaining that the chosen prefix equals its prefix. At each position, choose an allowed digit smaller than the bound's digit: the prefix is now strictly smaller, so every suffix of the remaining length is unrestricted. Add \u003ccode>count_smaller * d^remaining\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If the bound's digit is allowed, continue along the unique equal-prefix branch. Otherwise that branch cannot represent a valid number and must stop. If all positions are matched, add one for the bound itself. The cases are disjoint: every smaller equal-length number has a unique first position where it differs. They are also exhaustive, because at that first difference the candidate's digit must be smaller. The shorter cases and the bound itself are separate from these branches, proving the count.\u003c/p>\u003cp>No sorted input is required: counting comparisons against each digit yields the same number regardless of order. An empty set, though outside the usual constraints, produces zero. The procedure uses no recursion or state per candidate and does not rely on generating the actual numbers. Leading zeros require a different treatment; they are excluded by this interface rather than silently counted as full-length representations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For digits \u003ccode>['1', '3']\u003c/code> and bound 31:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Shorter lengths\u003c/p>\u003cpre>There are 2 one-digit choices: 1 and 3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First digit of 31\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Choosing 1 instead of 3 leaves two choices for the last digit, contributing 11 and 13. Digit 3 is allowed, so follow the equal branch.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second digit and equality\u003c/p>\u003cpre>No allowed digit is below 1. Digit 1 is allowed, so add the bound 31 itself. Total: 2 + 2 + 1 = 5.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If the first bound digit were absent from the allowed set, all smaller first-digit branches would still be counted before the equality branch stopped.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_allowed_numbers(digits, bound):\n    if bound &lt;= 0 or not digits:\n        return 0\n    text = str(bound)\n    d = len(digits)\n    total = sum(d ** length for length in range(1, len(text)))\n    for i, current in enumerate(text):\n        smaller = sum(digit &lt; current for digit in digits)\n        total += smaller * d ** (len(text) - i - 1)\n        if current not in digits:\n            return total\n    return total + 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With L bound digits and d allowed digits, there are O(Ld) digit comparisons and O(L) power expressions. Under the fixed official bound and digit alphabet, this is O(Ld) time and O(L) space including the decimal string; integer exponentiation and arbitrary-precision arithmetic are not literally constant time for unbounded L. No array proportional to the bound or number of answers is allocated.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not count zero: all allowed digits are nonzero and positive lengths start at one. Include the bound only if every one of its digits is allowed. At a missing equal-prefix digit, add the smaller branches first and then stop. Reuse of digits is allowed, so suffix choices are powers rather than permutations. The interface assumes unique digit characters; duplicates would incorrectly multiply counts. A digit set containing zero needs a separate leading-digit rule.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count valid values in an inclusive interval by subtracting counts up to one below its lower endpoint.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Permit zero in the set by treating the first digit separately from later positions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:ac:dp_s":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a positive decimal integer K as a string and a positive divisor D, count integers from 1 through K whose decimal digit sums are divisible by D. Return the count modulo 1,000,000,007. For K = \u003ccode>\"20\"\u003c/code> and D = 2, the qualifying numbers include 2, 4, 6, 8, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19, and 20, giving 10.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>K can contain up to 10,000 digits, and 1 &lt;= D &lt;= 100. Converting K to a machine integer or recursing once per digit is unnecessary and may fail in Python. Its representation has no leading zero and K is positive. Only the remainder of the digit sum matters, so two rolling arrays of D counts suffice for each tightness category.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the integers from 1 to K, where K has up to 10,000 digits, whose digit sum is a multiple of D, modulo 10^9 + 7.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You can never list the numbers, only choose their digits from the left. What is the least you must remember about the digits chosen so far?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Digit DP with a tight flag\u003c/p>\u003cp>Digit DP over (position, digit sum mod D, tight), filled as a table over K's digits rather than by recursion; it also counts 0, so subtract 1 and reduce modulo p.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(len(K) · D · 10) time, O(D) space per position\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For a small numeric bound, loop from 1 to K, sum each number's decimal digits, and count remainders equal to zero. This uses O(K · L) time for L digits and O(L) conversion space. It is infeasible for a 10,000-digit bound but is independent enough to test the digit-state implementation on short inputs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Pad all candidates with leading zeros to the length of K. Each integer from 0 to K then has exactly one padded representation, and padding does not change digit sum. Process positions from left to right, recording the sum modulo D and whether the chosen prefix is exactly equal to K's prefix. A tight state may choose at most the current bound digit; a loose state may choose any digit. The new state remains tight only when the old state was tight and the selected digit equals the bound digit.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every legal padded prefix has exactly one predecessor and last digit. The transitions enumerate those pairs once, so induction gives the correct count for every state. At the end, sum both tightness categories with remainder zero. The all-zero representation is included exactly once; subtract it to restrict the count to positive integers, then reduce modulo the modulus. Leading zeros are safe here because they contribute nothing to the sum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For K = \u003ccode>\"12\"\u003c/code>, D = 3:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First digit\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Choosing 0 creates a loose prefix with remainder 0; choosing 1 creates a tight prefix with remainder 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second digit\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The loose prefix can choose 0 through 9, producing remainder zero for 00, 03, 06, and 09. The tight prefix may choose 0 through 2, adding 12.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove zero\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Five padded representations qualify including 00; subtract one to return 4 for 3, 6, 9, and 12.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_digit_sum_multiples(bound, divisor):\n    mod = 1_000_000_007\n    loose = [0] * divisor\n    tight = [0] * divisor\n    tight[0] = 1\n    for char in bound:\n        limit = int(char)\n        next_loose = [0] * divisor\n        next_tight = [0] * divisor\n        for remainder in range(divisor):\n            for digit in range(10):\n                target = (remainder + digit) % divisor\n                next_loose[target] = (next_loose[target] + loose[remainder]) % mod\n            for digit in range(limit + 1):\n                target = (remainder + digit) % divisor\n                if digit == limit:\n                    next_tight[target] = (next_tight[target] + tight[remainder]) % mod\n                else:\n                    next_loose[target] = (next_loose[target] + tight[remainder]) % mod\n        loose, tight = next_loose, next_tight\n    return (loose[0] + tight[0] - 1) % mod\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For L decimal digits, each position updates at most 20D digit transitions, giving O(L D · 10) time and O(D) auxiliary space, excluding the input string. Counts are reduced throughout so they remain bounded. The explicit iteration handles long bounds without a recursion limit or integer conversion.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Subtract zero once, not once per leading-zero length. Preserve tightness only for an equal digit selected from a tight prefix; a loose prefix never becomes tight again. When D is 1, every positive number qualifies, so the answer is K modulo the modulus. Reducing the subtraction handles a negative intermediate residue correctly. The contract requires a positive divisor and a valid nonnegative decimal string.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Subtract the counts for two bounds to answer an inclusive range query, taking care at the lower endpoint.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Track another finite-state property, such as a forbidden adjacent digit pair, alongside the remainder.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:943":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a collection of words, return any shortest string containing every word as a contiguous substring. The same character positions may serve several words when their endings and beginnings overlap. For \u003ccode>['ab', 'bc', 'ca']\u003c/code>, one valid shortest answer is \u003ccode>abca\u003c/code>; other equally short answers are acceptable. The output is the string itself, not just its length. Input order does not constrain the order in the result.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are at most 12 nonempty lowercase words, each of length at most 20. The standard instance excludes a word contained inside another; the implementation also removes duplicates and contained words safely before solving. At most 12 independent words makes a subset-and-last-word dynamic program feasible, whereas trying all orderings becomes expensive. If the collection is empty, the function returns the empty string as a useful extension.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find a shortest string that contains each of up to 12 given words.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Writing the words one after another, each consecutive pair saves its overlap. Which famous ordering problem is that?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Bitmask DP over subsets\u003c/p>\u003cp>Precompute overlap[i][j]; dp[mask][last] is the largest total overlap of an order of the words in mask ending with last. Rebuild the order from parent pointers and glue the words, skipping each overlap.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n² · 2^n + n² · L) time, O(n · 2^n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>After removing redundant contained words, enumerate all permutations. For each ordering, append each word using its longest suffix-prefix overlap with the previous one, and keep the shortest result. There are n! orders; a direct overlap scan takes O(L²) per pair with ordinary string slices, for O(n! · n L²) time and O(nL) working space apart from permutation generation. A different independent oracle can enumerate short candidate strings by length and test substring membership, avoiding dependence on the ordering argument.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First deduplicate and remove any word contained in another. This preserves the set of valid superstrings. For remaining words compute \u003ccode>overlap[i][j]\u003c/code>, the largest number of trailing characters of word i equal to leading characters of word j. Appending j after i then costs \u003ccode>len(j) - overlap[i][j]\u003c/code> new characters. The state \u003ccode>dp[mask][last]\u003c/code> is the maximum total saved overlap of an ordering of exactly the words in the mask ending in \u003ccode>last\u003c/code>. Extend it with an unused word and add the corresponding pair overlap; save a predecessor on improvement.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why do orderings suffice? In any superstring, select an occurrence of each remaining word and sort them by starting position. Since no remaining word is contained in another, their ending positions also strictly increase. Consecutive occurrences overlap by a suffix-prefix match, or have a gap. Replace each gap by zero characters and replace each actual overlap with the largest possible overlap for that pair. Gluing in this order produces a superstring no longer than the original. Thus at least one ordering reaches the optimum. Conversely every glued ordering is a valid superstring, because appending preserves the previous word and creates the next one. Maximizing savings over all orderings therefore minimizes the resulting length.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each state the previous word and mask summarize everything needed to price a next append. Every ordering has one predecessor obtained by removing its last word, so the recurrence considers all orders. Setting a bit increases the mask; processing masks in increasing order makes each predecessor available. Parent pointers reconstruct a word order, which is then glued using the precomputed overlaps. Ties may return any optimum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>['ab', 'bc', 'ca']\u003c/code>, each neighboring pair in the cycle has overlap one:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Singletons\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Each one-word state has total overlap 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Extend\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The order ab -&gt; bc saves one character and produces abc. Extending it with ca saves one more character.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reconstruct\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The order ab -&gt; bc -&gt; ca glues to abca with total overlap 2 and length 2 + 2 + 2 - 2 = 4.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>No three-character string can contain all three distinct length-two words: it has only two length-two windows. Thus this result is shortest, although another ordering may give another optimum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def shortest_superstring(words):\n    unique = list(dict.fromkeys(words))\n    words = [word for word in unique\n             if not any(word != other and word in other for other in unique)]\n    n = len(words)\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"\"\n    overlap = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            if i == j:\n                continue\n            for k in range(min(len(words[i]), len(words[j])), 0, -1):\n                if words[i][-k:] == words[j][:k]:\n                    overlap[i][j] = k\n                    break\n    dp = [[-1] * n for _ in range(1 &lt;&lt; n)]\n    parent = [[-1] * n for _ in range(1 &lt;&lt; n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[1 &lt;&lt; i][i] = 0\n    for mask in range(1 &lt;&lt; n):\n        for last in range(n):\n            if dp[mask][last] &lt; 0:\n                continue\n            for nxt in range(n):\n                if mask &amp; (1 &lt;&lt; nxt):\n                    continue\n                new_mask = mask | (1 &lt;&lt; nxt)\n                candidate = dp[mask][last] + overlap[last][nxt]\n                if candidate &gt; dp[new_mask][nxt]:\n                    dp[new_mask][nxt] = candidate\n                    parent[new_mask][nxt] = last\n    mask = (1 &lt;&lt; n) - 1\n    last = max(range(n), key=lambda i: dp[mask][i])\n    order = []\n    while last != -1:\n        order.append(last)\n        previous = parent[mask][last]\n        mask ^= 1 &lt;&lt; last\n        last = previous\n    order.reverse()\n    result = words[order[0]]\n    for i, j in zip(order, order[1:]):\n        result += words[j][overlap[i][j]:]\n    return result\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let n be the number of input words and L their maximum length; filtering can only reduce n. The naive substring filtering and overlap comparisons take O(n² L²) time in the worst case with ordinary string matching and slicing. The subset transitions take O(n² 2^n) time, and reconstructing the result takes at most O(n² L) time with repeated immutable-string concatenation. Space is O(n 2^n + n² + nL), including tables, overlaps, retained words and output. These bounds reflect this particular code rather than assuming a linear-time string-matching primitive.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A word already inside another should not be forced into the order as a separate endpoint; remove it first. Handle duplicates before that removal so identical words do not eliminate each other. An overlap is a suffix of the previous word and a prefix of the next, not an arbitrary shared substring. Use \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code> for unreachable states, because zero overlap is a valid reachable score. A single word returns itself, and an empty collection returns an empty string. Multiple shortest answers are normal; test length and containment rather than one fixed spelling.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the minimum length only, dropping predecessor storage and reconstruction.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Attach directional costs to transitions and solve a different subset-ordering problem using the same state structure.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2376":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count positive integers no greater than a given positive bound whose decimal representations have no repeated digit. For bound 20, all positive integers except 11 qualify, so the answer is 19. Zero is not included. A repeated zero matters after a number has started: 100 is invalid, whereas the padding zeros before a shorter number are not part of its representation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The bound is between 1 and 2,000,000,000, so it has at most ten decimal digits. A ten-bit mask can record which digits have occurred. A separate started flag distinguishes the number's real first digit from leading padding. The equality flag restricts a prefix that still matches the bound. There are only a fixed number of masks per position, even though the number of candidate integers can be huge.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the integers from 1 to n whose digits are all different.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Build numbers from the left. What must you remember about the digits so far, and how do you stop leading zeros counting as used digits?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Digit DP with a tight flag\u003c/p>\u003cp>Digit DP over (position, used-digit mask, tight, started): leading zeros keep started false and leave the mask alone; a digit is allowed only if its bit is not set; count the states that end with started true.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(L · 2^10 · 10) time, O(L · 2^10) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Inspect every integer from 1 through the bound, convert it to decimal, and compare the number of distinct characters to its length. This takes O(bound · L) time for L decimal positions and O(L) temporary space. It is a useful independent oracle for small bounds, but scanning billions of candidates is too costly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Represent each integer with exactly L positions by padding on the left with zeros. Keep counts indexed by \u003ccode>(mask, tight, started)\u003c/code> after each position. The mask contains only digits of the actual representation. While \u003ccode>started\u003c/code> is false, choosing zero leaves the mask empty and still represents padding. Choosing a nonzero digit starts the number and sets that digit's bit. Once started, any digit including zero is legal only if its bit is absent, after which that bit is set.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If tight is true, the next digit cannot exceed the corresponding bound digit; otherwise any decimal digit can be selected. Tight remains true exactly when the old prefix was tight and the new digit equals the bound digit. At the end sum counts whose started flag is true, excluding the unique all-padding representation of zero.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every number in the range has one padded representation, and every transition corresponds to one next digit. Inductively each state count is exactly the number of legal prefixes with its mask and flags. Rejecting a used bit forbids precisely the repeated-digit prefixes; leading zeros do not reserve the zero bit until the number actually begins. Consequently the final sum counts every valid positive integer exactly once. Use a fresh mapping for each digit so newly created states cannot be consumed again in the same position.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For bound 20, consider its two positions:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First position\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Choosing 0 leaves an unstarted loose prefix; choosing 1 creates a loose prefix with mask {1}; choosing 2 creates a tight prefix with mask {2}.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second position\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The unstarted branch yields the nine one-digit positives. The 1 branch yields ten choices except another 1, adding nine two-digit values.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bound branch\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The tight 2 branch permits only digit 0, producing 20. The all-padding zero is excluded, so 9 + 9 + 1 = 19.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The 0 chosen after the leading 2 is a real digit and sets its bit. By contrast, the first-position 0 on the shorter-number branch does not.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_distinct_digit_numbers(bound):\n    if bound &lt;= 0:\n        return 0\n    states = {(0, True, False): 1}\n    for char in str(bound):\n        limit = int(char)\n        following = {}\n        for (mask, tight, started), count in states.items():\n            upper = limit if tight else 9\n            for digit in range(upper + 1):\n                new_tight = tight and digit == limit\n                if not started and digit == 0:\n                    key = (mask, new_tight, False)\n                else:\n                    bit = 1 &lt;&lt; digit\n                    if mask &amp; bit:\n                        continue\n                    key = (mask | bit, new_tight, True)\n                following[key] = following.get(key, 0) + count\n        states = following\n    return sum(count for (_, _, started), count in states.items() if started)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At each of L positions there are at most 2^10 masks and four flag combinations, each trying at most ten digits. Time is O(L · 2^10 · 10), and the two rolling mappings use O(2^10) auxiliary space, apart from the O(L) string conversion. The official bound keeps counts small enough for ordinary integer operations; no modulus is required.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not mark leading padding zeros as used digits: doing so would incorrectly reject numbers such as 10. Conversely, after starting, zero is a normal digit and a second zero is forbidden. Do not count the all-padding state. A loose state cannot become tight again. A one-digit bound counts every positive value through that bound; a nonpositive bound returns zero in this extended interface. Repeated digits in the bound do not themselves invalidate smaller candidates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count valid integers in a range by subtracting prefix counts, including the lower endpoint carefully.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a remainder state to require the number itself to be divisible by a chosen modulus.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
